'MegaNet -- base-station-http.CR300
'
'   A radio base station's half of MegaNet's ingest: read what the ALERT2
'   receiver hears off RS-232, and send it to MegaNet over BOTH inbound paths --
'   POST to meganet.ingest_http(), and publish to the MQTT bridge.
'
' Target: CR300-series. "Other dataloggers" at the foot says what to change for
' a CR1000X or a CR6. docs/ingest-http.md and docs/ingest-mqtt.md are the two
' contracts this program has to satisfy; logger/README.md is the commissioning
' guide, including the Device Configuration Utility settings that have to be
' right before either half of this can deliver anything at all.
'
' --- What this is -----------------------------------------------------------
'
' MegaNet has two inbound paths and they converge on one contract. HTTP needs no
' process anywhere: the datalogger posts straight at the database. MQTT needs a
' broker and bridge/ -- a subscriber that holds the subscription Postgres cannot
' hold -- and pays for them with what it gives back: the broker's Last Will says
' when this base station stopped talking, which no amount of HTTP tells anybody.
'
' This program does both, for the same readings, from one queue:
'
'   field stations --radio--> ERT-A2 receiver --RS-232--> THIS PROGRAM
'                                                            |
'                                          +-----------------+-----------------+
'                                          |                                   |
'                                        HTTPS                         MQTT (QoS 1)
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          v                                   v
'                               meganet.ingest_http()                       broker
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          |                                bridge/
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          +-----------------+-----------------+
'                                                            |
'                                                    meganet.ingest()
'
' **Sending the same reading twice is not a mistake, it is the design.**
' meganet.reading's primary key is (address, instant, value), so the second copy
' of a reading is stored zero times and counted as a duplicate -- both contracts
' say so in as many words, which is what makes a belt-and-braces base station
' possible without an acknowledgement protocol between the two paths. Whichever
' copy lands first sets the row's `source` ("http" or "mqtt"); the other is
' counted and discarded. A path being down costs nothing but its own duplicates.
'
' One token and one broker credential, one base station, every station it can
' hear -- docs/ingest-http.md explains why the token belongs to the ingest point
' rather than to a site, and the same reasoning gives this base station one
' publisher identity of its own on the topic scheme.
'
' --- How the serial port is read, and why it changed ------------------------
'
' The first version of this program called SerialInRecord() once per scan in a
' drain loop. That was the bug. SerialInRecord's last parameter is
' RecordsBackFromNewest -- how many records back from the most recent one to
' hand over -- and it was being passed 1, which asks for the record BEFORE the
' newest and returns nothing at all when only one has arrived. It is also the
' wrong shape for draining: one call is meant to yield one record per scan, so
' looping on it does not empty a buffer that filled between scans.
'
' This version reads the port the way the KDO doppler driver on our other
' loggers reads its sensor, which is the pattern that is known to work on this
' hardware:
'
'   1. SerialInChk()   -- how many bytes are sitting in the port buffer
'   2. SerialInBlock() -- take all of them, raw, and it returns the byte count
'   3. walk the block byte by byte with ASCII(), building a padded hex dump and
'      a printable copy with control characters shown as '-'
'   4. append the bytes to an assembly buffer and cut complete lines out of it
'      on CR or LF
'
' Nothing about that depends on an option code whose meaning has to be looked
' up, and every step of it is visible in the Public table -- see section 2. The
' byte count from SerialInBlock is what bounds the walk, not Len(), because the
' port is open in transparent mode and a NUL byte would end a CRBasic string
' early. NULs are skipped and counted rather than silently truncating a line.
'
' --- The four things this program is careful about --------------------------
'
' 1. It never blocks the scan on the network. Serial is read in the 1-second
'    main scan; the POST happens in a SlowSequence, because HTTPPost sits there
'    until the server answers and a base station that stops reading its serial
'    port for a minute has lost every transmission in that window.
'
' 2. It never throws a reading away because a link was down. Parsed readings go
'    into a ring buffer and stay there until they have been delivered. A failed
'    attempt is retried with backoff and the same batch is sent again -- both
'    contracts guarantee that re-sending the same reading stores it once, so
'    there is no acknowledgement protocol to build here.
'
'    The buffer has TWO readers, one per path, each with its own position in it,
'    its own backoff and its own idea of what it has delivered. A slot is only
'    reused once BOTH have passed it. That is what stops a broker outage from
'    stalling the POSTs, an HTTP outage from stalling the publishes, and -- the
'    failure a single shared position would have caused -- a stuck path from
'    pinning the other one to the same twenty-five readings while newer ones
'    pile up behind it and fall off the end unsent.
'
' 3. It does not trust a clock it cannot check. The endpoint rejects anything
'    stamped before 1990, and the usual cause is an RTC that lost power. This
'    program disciplines its clock against NTP, and refuses to stamp a reading
'    until the clock passes a sanity check, rather than posting rubbish.
'
' 4. It does not convert. value_raw is the 11-bit number that came off the air.
'    A base station hearing forty sites does not know which of them is a rain
'    gauge and which is a level sensor, and a wrong bucket size is worse than
'    no bucket size -- MegaNet does the conversion where it knows the sensor.
'
' The token is not in this file and never appears in a Public variable. It is
' read at startup out of a file on the logger, so this program can live in a
' public repository, be emailed, and be loaded onto a second base station
' without carrying a live credential.
'
' --- A note on what is hand-rolled and what is not --------------------------
'
' The substring search and the line splitter are written out of Mid(), Len() and
' ASCII() rather than calling InStr() and SplitStr(). Both of those take an
' option code, and a wrong option code does not fail to compile -- it silently
' parses the wrong thing, which for a program whose entire job is parsing is the
' worst failure mode available. Bit fields are pulled out with MOD and INT
' rather than with shift operators, for the same reason: CRBasic has no << or >>
' and the arithmetic form is unambiguous on every operating system.
'
' This file is deliberately plain ASCII throughout, so it reads the same in
' CRBasic Editor as it does on GitHub.
'
'==============================================================================


'==============================================================================
' 1. SITE CONFIGURATION -- the part you edit
'==============================================================================

' Which ingest point this is is at the top of the Public table -- see
' BASE_NAME there. It is Public rather than Const so the name can be corrected
' at the logger without recompiling.

' The endpoint, from docs/ingest-http.md. https, so TLS turns itself on.
Const INGEST_URI = "https://jjprlritvhdqpvphfrnu.supabase.co/rest/v1/rpc/ingest_http"

' Identifies the Supabase project. Not a secret -- it is committed to this
' repository and can neither read nor write anything on its own.
Const API_KEY = "sb_publishable_PV9VjCM8NQeGAJMuwa5TKA_yX9GWacY"

' Where the secret lives. One line, the token, nothing else. Load it with Device
' Configuration Utility (File Control); see logger/README.md. If the file is
' missing the program still runs and still fills its Readings table -- it just
' says "no token" in PostState and keeps queueing. CPU: is used because a CR300
' has no microSD slot, so CRD: does not exist on this hardware.
Const TOKEN_FILE = "CPU:meganet_token.txt"

' The wire protocol behind the readings, recorded against every one of them.
' "alert2" for an ERT-A2. Change it to "alert" if your decoder is feeding this
' program legacy ALERT, or "unknown" if you would rather not claim.
Const PROTOCOL = "alert2"

' --- the serial port ---------------------------------------------------------
'
' 9600-8-N-1 is the ERT-A2's ALERT2 ASCII default. Format 3 is 8-N-1
' transparent, and it is the right one for a third-party feed: format 0 discards
' ASCII 0 and every byte above 127, and leaves the PakBus stack scanning the
' stream for frames it will never find. The KDO driver these constants are
' modelled on uses the same three values.
Const RX_PORT = ComRS232
Const RX_BAUD = 9600
Const RX_FORMAT = 3
Const RX_TXDELAY = 0
Const RX_BUFFER = 2000             'port buffer, bytes. Two seconds of 9600 baud
                                   'is 1920 bytes, so this holds a full scan's
                                   'worth twice over

Const RX_BLOCK = 250               'most bytes taken from the port in one read
Const RX_MAX = 250                 'longest text line this program will assemble

' How much of a block or a line to show in the hex and text diagnostics. The
' dump is 3 characters per byte, so 128 bytes is 384 characters. Longer than
' this and the diagnostic ends in "..." -- the parsing is not affected, only
' what you can read on screen.
Const DUMP_MAX = 128

' --- posting over HTTP -------------------------------------------------------

' How often to try a POST, and how many readings may go in one. The endpoint's
' own limit is 1000 per batch, which is not the binding one here: a reading is
' 69 characters, or 92 when it carries the suspect flag, so 25 of the longest
' possible readings plus the envelope come to 2366 -- comfortably inside
' CONTENT_GUARD below. 25 readings every 30 seconds is 50 a minute; a 40-station
' base reporting every 15 minutes produces about 8.
Const POST_EVERY = 30              'seconds between POST attempts
Const MAX_BATCH = 25               'readings per POST

' Retry backoff after a failed POST: doubles from BACKOFF_MIN up to BACKOFF_MAX.
Const BACKOFF_MIN = 30             'seconds
Const BACKOFF_MAX = 900            'seconds -- 15 minutes

' --- publishing over MQTT ----------------------------------------------------
'
' The second path. docs/ingest-mqtt.md is the contract; the broker connection
' itself is NOT configured here -- it is a block of Device Configuration Utility
' settings (broker URL, port, TLS, client id, username, password, clean session,
' keep alive, and the Last Will) that CRBasic can neither read nor set.
' logger/README.md lists every one of them and what to put in it. This section
' is only the part that belongs to the program: which topics, how often, and how
' hard to retry.
'
' Set MQTT_ENABLE False on a base station that has no broker credential, or on a
' logger whose operating system predates MQTTPublish() -- on that logger the
' program will not compile at all and the MQTT subroutines have to be deleted,
' which is the one change this flag cannot make for you.
Const MQTT_ENABLE = True

' WHO THIS BASE STATION IS ON THE WIRE. The <station> topic segment: the BUREAU
' STATION NUMBER, exactly as meganet.station.station_number holds it -- `541155`,
' not `0541155`. Sites that have no bureau number publish under their station id
' instead, which is one rule rather than two identifiers, and a base station
' never has a bureau number: it is an ingest point, not a gauging station.
'
' "18 Bateson" is stations.json id `18_bateson`, station_number "". So the id is
' what goes here, and it is what the broker ACL for this credential spells --
' change BOTH together or the broker refuses the publish, which is the loud
' failure the scheme is built to produce (docs/ingest-mqtt.md, 0020).
'
' Letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore only, starting with a letter or a
' digit, up to 64 characters. bridge/src/topics.js is the same rule in code.
Const MQTT_STATION = "18_bateson"

' WHICH BOX AT THE SITE IS TALKING. `logger` for the usual case of one. It says
' who published, not what was measured -- the readings carry their own
' addresses, which is what MegaNet stores against.
Const MQTT_DEVICE = "logger"

' QoS 1, not 2. At-least-once means duplicates are guaranteed, and the primary
' key already eats them; exactly-once costs two extra round trips per message
' over a marginal link and buys nothing. QoS 2 is not supported by the
' instruction in any case.
'
' MQTTPublish() BLOCKS at QoS 1 -- it waits for the broker's PUBACK or a timeout
' -- which is why it lives in the same SlowSequence as HTTPPost and not in the
' scan that reads the serial port.
Const MQTT_QOS = 1

' Retain flags, written as the numbers the instruction takes rather than as True
' and False: CRBasic's True is -1, and -1 in a retain parameter is not something
' to find out about from a broker.
Const MQTT_KEEP = 1                'retained -- the status topic
Const MQTT_DROP = 0                'not retained -- readings

' How often to publish, and the retry backoff, with the same shape and the same
' defaults as the POST above. They are separate constants rather than shared
' ones because the two paths fail independently and at different rates: a
' cellular link that is too poor for a 2 KB POST body will often still carry a
' publish, and tuning one should not move the other.
Const MQTT_EVERY = 30              'seconds between publish attempts
Const MQTT_BACKOFF_MIN = 30        'seconds
Const MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX = 900       'seconds -- 15 minutes

' How often to re-publish the retained status message.
'
' This is the half of MQTT that is arguably worth more than the readings.
' "Which sites stopped talking overnight" is the morning question, and the
' broker answers it with the Last Will -- set in Device Configuration Utility,
' published by the BROKER on this station's behalf when the connection drops
' without a clean disconnect, which is exactly the case a station cannot report
' itself. All this program has to do is publish the other half: a retained
' {"online": true, ...} saying it is here.
'
' Re-published on a timer rather than only at startup because a logger that
' reconnects after an outage has to overwrite the will the broker published for
' it, and there is no connect event in CRBasic to hang that on. Fifteen minutes
' is one small retained message an hour and a bit -- cheap on any link that can
' carry readings at all.
Const MQTT_STATUS_EVERY = 900      'seconds

' Reported in the status message, so "which loggers are still on the old
' program" is answerable from the database rather than from a site visit. Bump
' it when you change this file in a way a field technician would care about.
Const FW_VERSION = "2.0"

' Clock discipline. NTPOffset is seconds of offset from UTC, so 0 means the
' logger's clock runs in UTC -- which is what lets the timestamps below end in
' Z and mean it. Blank NTP_SERVER to turn this off and set the clock by hand.
Const NTP_SERVER = "au.pool.ntp.org"
Const NTP_EVERY_HR = 6

' Refuse to stamp a reading until NTP has succeeded at least once.
'
' This exists because of a real failure, not a hypothetical one. A logger whose
' clock was left on local time stamped every reading with the right digits and
' the wrong instant -- readings landed ten hours in the future, the endpoint
' accepted them (its ceiling is a day), and they were invisible in the app
' because every time window there ends at now. Nothing downstream can detect
' that, and nothing can undo it afterwards.
'
' The old code only synced on the NTP_EVERY_HR boundary, so a logger could run
' for hours before its first sync. It now syncs on the first pass and every
' NTP_EVERY_HR after that, and REQUIRE_NTP makes an unverified clock refuse to
' stamp rather than stamp something plausible.
'
' Set it False only if this logger genuinely cannot reach an NTP server -- then
' set the clock to UTC by hand and accept that nothing is checking it. Blanking
' NTP_SERVER disables the requirement too, for the same reason.
Const REQUIRE_NTP = True

' How much unsent data to hold. Each slot is one reading; 250 slots is roughly
' two hours for a busy 40-station base. When it fills, the OLDEST reading is
' dropped and QDropped counts it -- for flood warning the newest river level
' matters more than the one before it.
Const Q_SIZE = 250

' The JSON body buffer, and the length at which BuildBatch stops adding. Raise
' both together if you raise MAX_BATCH, and leave at least 92 characters -- one
' longest-case reading -- between the guard and the buffer, or the last append
' can still overrun.
Const CONTENT_SIZE = 3000
Const CONTENT_GUARD = 2700

' A reading whose 11-bit value is all ones. alert2.js calls this out as
' over-range or a dead sensor, so it is posted with quality "suspect" rather
' than dropped -- a stuck sensor is a fact about the network, not a bad row.
Const FULL_SCALE = 2047

' The clock sanity floor. The endpoint rejects anything before 1990 as a dead
' clock rather than a reading; this refuses to stamp one in the first place. Set
' above 1990 on purpose: a logger whose clock has reset can land just after
' 1990 and pass a literal 1990 test while still being meaningless.
Const MIN_YEAR = 2020

' The ALERT2 ASCII protocol's fixed fields, before the payload hex begins, and
' the most fields this program will split a line into.
Const N_FIELDS = 24
Const FIELD_MAX = 60

' --- the raw capture ---------------------------------------------------------
'
' A plain text file of every byte the port delivers, one text line per read,
' written so it can be pulled off the logger with Device Configuration Utility
' (File Control > Retrieve) -- the same route the token file goes on by. This is
' the thing to send when a feed is not doing what it should: it is lossless, it
' is before any parsing, and it survives the program being wrong.
'
'   # MegaNet base-station serial capture -- ComRS232 9600 format 3
'   # started 2026-08-17T04:15:00Z (logger clock, UTC)
'   04:15:07 n=31 41 4C 45 52 54 32 41 2C 31 2C ...
'
' Writes are batched: bytes accumulate in a buffer and go to flash when it is
' nearly full or every CAP_FLUSH_SEC, rather than once a second. Flash on a
' CR300 is not infinite, and a capture left running for a week at one write a
' second is a real cost -- so the file also stops itself at CAPTURE_MAX bytes.
Const CAPTURE_FILE = "CPU:rxcapture.txt"
Const CAPTURE_MAX = 200000         'bytes, then it stops and says so. Lower this
                                   'if CPU: is short of room -- 200 KB is about
                                   'nine hours of a busy 40-station base
Const CAP_BUF = 2000               'held in RAM between flushes
Const CAP_FLUSH_SEC = 60           'flush at least this often, so a capture cut
                                   'short by a power cycle loses a minute, not
                                   'everything since it started

' Payload byte 0 of an ALERT2 "ALERT concentration" element. Decimal 116, which
' is 0x74 -- written in decimal so there is no doubt what the compiler read.
Const ELEM_CONCENTRATION = 116

' --- the ERT-A2's BINARY framing ---------------------------------------------
'
' The receiver on this base station does not emit the ALERT2 ASCII lines above.
' It emits a binary frame, and a terminal capture of three of them settles the
' layout completely:
'
'   41 4C 45 52 54 32 | 56 | 9C 2C 02 00 01 75 ... 84 01 0B 74 5C 9E 87 09 8C 00
'   |___ "ALERT2" ___|  ^                          |_ anchor _||__ element ____|
'                       byte 7 is a LENGTH, not the "A" of ALERT2A
'
'   total bytes = 6 + byte 7.  0x56 -> 92 bytes, 0x52 -> 88 bytes, both exact.
'
' Byte 7 read as text is "V" or "R", which is why a terminal shows ALERT2V and
' ALERT2R and why this program, looking for the literal text ALERT2A, never
' matched one. The ASCII form is still recognised -- see the mode test in
' TakeFrames -- because "ALERT2A," is a letter A followed by a comma and a
' length byte cannot be.
'
' Inside the frame, the ALERT concentration element sits behind a fixed anchor:
'
'   84 01 <len> 74 <time hi> <time lo> then <len-3>/4 four-byte records
'
' which appeared at the same place in all three reference frames and decoded to
' sensible readings (alert_id 2438/2439 and 4134). The records are the same
' four-byte shape the ASCII path already decodes, so ParseAlert2's table applies
' unchanged.
Const SIG_LEN = 6                  'the length of "ALERT2"
Const BUF_MAX = 600                'the byte buffer, in bytes. Two frames and
                                   'change, which is all a 1-second scan can see
Const FRAME_MAX = 300              'a length byte claiming more than this is not
                                   'a length byte
Const FRAME_MIN_LEN = 12           'nor is one claiming less than this
Const FRAME_LOOPS = 24             'most frames or lines taken from the buffer in
                                   'one scan. A bound rather than a budget: the
                                   'buffer holds two frames, so reaching this
                                   'means something is looping that should not


'==============================================================================
' 2. DIAGNOSTICS -- what a technician reads in the Public table
'==============================================================================
'
' Everything below is visible in LoggerNet, PC400 or Device Configuration
' Utility without collecting a table.
'
' The serial block is the important part of this section and it is laid out as
' the pipeline it is: seven numbered steps from "bytes in the port buffer" to
' "the JSON that will be posted", each one showing the same data in the form it
' has reached. When something is wrong with a feed, the step where the display
' stops being what you expected is the step that is broken.

'--- which ingest point this is ----------------------------------------------
'
' Lands in every reading's `path` column and is what the Message Log tab shows
' as "which base heard it", so name it for the place a person standing there
' would recognise -- not for a field station it relays, because a base hears
' forty of those.
'
' Public rather than Const, so it can be corrected at the logger without a
' recompile. Two things follow. The value here is the default at every program
' start, not a saved setting, so the permanent home for a site's name is still
' this line. And the batch envelope is rebuilt per POST rather than once in
' BeginProg, so a name changed on the bench takes effect on the next post
' instead of at the next restart.
Public BASE_NAME As String * 20 = "18 Bateson"

'--- is the logger alive ------------------------------------------------------
Public Batt_volt                   'V -- station supply
Public PTemp                       'degC -- panel temperature
Public ClockOK As Boolean          'false = clock failed its sanity check, and
                                   'nothing is being stamped or queued
Public NowISO As String * 24       'the clock, formatted exactly as it is posted
Public NTPErrMs                    'clock error NTP found, in ms, before it
                                   'corrected it. Growing between syncs is drift
Public NTPLastOK As Boolean        'the last NTP sync succeeded
Public ClockVerified As Boolean    'NTP has succeeded at least once since this
                                   'program started, so the Z on every stamp is
                                   'the truth rather than a claim
Public ClockState As String * 60   'plain English: what the clock is doing, or
                                   'why nothing is being stamped
Public SecsSinceNTP

' Status table fields, read straight out of the Status table with
' Status.Fieldname. These three are on the CR300's own Status table; several
' familiar CR1000X fields (MemoryFree, SkippedSlowScan, ProgErrors) are NOT,
' which is why they are absent here rather than left to fail at compile time.
Public WatchdogErrs                'non-zero means the logger has been resetting
Public SkippedScans                'the main scan overrunning its second
Public VarOutOfBound               'an array index that went past its dimension

'--- SERIAL, STEP 1: the port ------------------------------------------------
Public SecsSinceRx                 'seconds since the last byte arrived. THE
                                   'number: a base station that has gone deaf
                                   'shows up here and nowhere else, because a
                                   'deaf base station is not an error, it is a
                                   'silence
Public RxAvail                     'bytes waiting in the port buffer, read with
                                   'SerialInChk BEFORE this scan's read. Should
                                   'sit near zero; parked near RX_BUFFER means
                                   'the scan is not keeping up
Public RxBlockBytes                'bytes SerialInBlock returned this scan
Public RxBytesTotal                'bytes read since startup
Public RxNulls                     'NUL bytes seen and skipped. Non-zero on an
                                   'ASCII feed means line noise or a baud
                                   'mismatch, and is the first thing to suspect
                                   'when RxBadFrames climbs with no obvious cause

'--- SERIAL, STEP 2: this scan's block, two ways -----------------------------
Public RxBlockHex As String * 400  'every byte of it as padded hex, exactly as
                                   'it came off the wire. This is the raw truth
                                   'and it is the display to trust
Public RxBlockHexFull As String * 620 'the same, never truncated, for RawLog
Public RxBufLenBefore              'buffer occupancy before this block went in
Public RxBlockText As String * 200 'the same bytes as text, with every control
                                   'character shown as '-' so nothing in the
                                   'data can scramble the display

'--- SERIAL, STEP 3: the byte buffer -----------------------------------------
'
' Bytes go into a numeric array, not a string, and NOTHING is removed from them.
' The first version of this program stripped NUL bytes before assembling lines,
' because a CRBasic string ends at the first NUL -- and the reference frames
' carry nine and eleven NULs each, so that alone destroyed every frame. It then
' cut lines on CR, and 0x0D occurs inside these frames as ordinary data. A
' numeric array has neither problem: a byte is a number and 0 is a number.
Public RxBufLen                    'bytes waiting to be framed
Public RxBufHex As String * 400    'what is waiting, in hex
Public RxBufText As String * 200   'and as text, printable-safe
Public RxLines                     'complete TEXT lines cut out since startup
Public RxNonText                   'runs discarded as not-text -- binary that
                                   'happened to contain a CR or LF
Public RxBufDrops                  'times the buffer filled with nothing framable
                                   'in it and had to be discarded
Public RxBlockSeq As Long          'increments on every serial read, so RawLog
                                   'rows can be lined up against FrameLog rows

'--- SERIAL, STEP 3b: the binary frame ---------------------------------------
Public RxFrameMode As String * 8   'ascii or binary, decided per frame
Public RxFrameSeq As Long          'increments per frame found
Public RxFrameBadLen               'signatures whose length byte was implausible
Public FrPre                       'bytes that sat in front of the signature and
                                   'were discarded -- non-zero means noise or a
                                   'partial previous frame
Public FrLenByte                   'byte 7, the length
Public FrTotal                     '6 + FrLenByte, the whole frame
Public FrLeftover                  'bytes still in the buffer after the frame was
                                   'taken. Near zero confirms the length rule
Public FrHex As String * 400       'the entire frame, in hex
Public FrElemVia                   'how the concentration element was found:
                                   '1 the 84 01 len 74 anchor, 2 a loose scan,
                                   '0 not found at all
Public FrElemOff                   'where it starts, counted from the frame start
Public FrElemLen                   'its length byte
Public FrElemHex As String * 120   'the element itself, in hex
Public FrTimeSecs                  'the two time bytes inside it
Public FrRecords                   'four-byte records it carries
Public FrGood                      'of those, how many decoded
Public FrBad                       'and how many had a non-zero status byte or an
                                   'out-of-range address

'--- SERIAL, STEP 3c: one reading, byte by byte ------------------------------
Public RdIndex                     'which record within the frame
Public RdB0 As Long                'the four bytes, as numbers, so the
Public RdB1 As Long                'bit-unpacking can be checked by hand against
Public RdB2 As Long                'the table at ParseAlert2. Long, not the
Public RdB3 As Long                'default float: Sprintf's %02X reads a float
                                   'as zero, which is why RdRecHex printed
                                   '"00 00 00 00" beside four correct numbers
Public RdRecHex As String * 12
Public RdQueued As Boolean

'--- SERIAL, STEP 4: the last complete line ----------------------------------
Public RxLastLine As String * 250  'verbatim, terminator stripped. Worth more
                                   'than any counter when the format is not what
                                   'you expected
Public RxLastLineHex As String * 400
Public RxLastLineLen
Public RxLastShape As String * 16  'what the line was taken to be: ALERT2A,
                                   'plain, or unrecognised
Public RxLastFields                'fields the splitter found in it
Public RxLastDelim As String * 12  'which separator the splitter used: comma,
                                   'semicolon, tab, pipe, space, or none. "none"
                                   'with a long line is the whole diagnosis of
                                   'an "unrecognised line" -- see RxLastSep
Public RxSepComma As Long          'the separator census as numbers, so LineLog
Public RxSepSpace As Long          'can carry it. RxLastSep below is the same
Public RxSepTab As Long            'thing as one readable string
Public RxSepSemi As Long
Public RxSepColon As Long
Public RxSepPipe As Long
Public RxSepEq As Long
Public RxSepHi As Long             'bytes above 126 -- not ASCII
Public RxSepCtl As Long            'control characters inside the line

' The most common non-alphanumeric character in the line, whatever it is. The
' counters above test a list this program guessed in advance; this one tests
' nothing and just reports what is actually there, which is the answer when a
' feed is delimited by something nobody thought of -- a slash, a dash, an
' asterisk. RxTopSepDec is its byte value, so it survives being a character no
' display can render.
Public RxTopSep As String * 4
Public RxTopSepDec As Long
Public RxTopSepN As Long

' The line itself, in two short printable-safe samples. These are what make
' LineLog answer a question on its own instead of only narrowing one down: a
' table of lengths and field counts says a feed has a shape, and the first forty
' characters of it say what the shape IS.
Public RxLastHead As String * 40   'the first 40 characters
Public RxLastTail As String * 16   'and the last 16, which is where checksums and
                                   'record terminators live

Public RxLastSep As String * 90    'the whole census as one readable string:
                                   '  comma=30 space=0 tab=0 semi=0 colon=0
                                   '  pipe=0 eq=0 hi=0 ctl=0 top=44 x30
                                   'This is the one line to read when a feed
                                   'will not parse. Every count zero means the
                                   'line is a single unbroken token; hi>0 means
                                   'bytes above 126, which is not ASCII at all
Public RxLastPrefix                'bytes discarded from the front of the line
                                   'to reach "ALERT2A". Non-zero means the
                                   'receiver prefixes its lines with something
Public RxLastDelimCode As Long     'the same as RxLastDelim, as a number, so the
Public RxLastShapeCode As Long     'LineLog table can carry them: delim 0 none,
                                   '1 comma, 2 semicolon, 3 tab, 4 pipe,
                                   '5 space; shape 1 ALERT2A, 2 plain,
                                   '3 unrecognised

'--- SERIAL, STEP 5: the decoded payload -------------------------------------
Public RxLastPayLen                'payload bytes, from field 23
Public RxLastPayHex As String * 100 'those bytes after the hex fields were
                                   'converted back to numbers -- if this does
                                   'not match the tail of RxLastLineHex, the
                                   'hex decode is what is wrong

'--- SERIAL, STEP 6: the last reading ----------------------------------------
Public RxLastRecHex As String * 12 'the four payload bytes this reading came out
                                   'of, so the bit-unpacking can be checked by
                                   'hand against the table at ParseAlert2
Public RxLastId As Long            'ALERT id decoded from them
Public RxLastValue As Long         'and the 11-bit value
Public RxLastSuspect As Boolean    'value was full scale
Public RxLastStamp As String * 24  'the stamp actually attached to it

'--- SERIAL, STEP 7: what will be posted -------------------------------------
Public RxLastJson As String * 120  'the JSON object for that reading, byte for
                                   'byte as it will appear in the batch

'--- how far the last line got -----------------------------------------------
Public RxStep As String * 40       'the last step the pipeline completed. Where
                                   'this stops is where to look
Public RxLastWhy As String * 60    'why the last rejected line was rejected
Public RxLastBad As String * 250   'and the line itself
Public RxLastBadHex As String * 400 'and that line in hex, which is the copy
                                   'that still means something when the text is
                                   'not printable
Public RxFrames                    'frames that decoded
Public RxReadings                  'readings extracted from them
Public RxBadFrames                 'lines rejected
Public RxBadRecords                'records inside good frames whose status byte
                                   'was non-zero
Public RxFrameSkew                 'seconds between the frame's own ALERT2 time
                                   'and this logger's clock -- see ParseAlert2.
                                   'Logged as IEEE4, not FP2: FP2 cannot hold a
                                   'number past 7999 and a whole-hour skew is
                                   'tens of thousands of seconds, so an FP2
                                   'column reports a clamped -7999 for anything
                                   'interesting

'--- SERIAL FORENSICS: what the whole feed looks like, not just the last line -
'
' These are what a capture is for. Left running for a while they answer whether
' the feed is ASCII at all, which terminator it uses, whether every line is the
' same length, and whether every rejection is the same rejection -- none of
' which a single last-line snapshot can tell you.

' Every byte received since startup, sorted into eight classes. This is the
' fastest read on the whole table: printable plus CR plus LF and nothing else is
' an ASCII feed; anything in `high` or `ctrl-other` is not, and no amount of
' parser work will make it one.
Public RxByteClass(8) As Long
Public RxByteClassName(8) As String * 12   'labels, filled in BeginProg

' Every line rejected since startup, by reason. All of them landing on one
' counter says the feed has a single consistent shape this program does not
' know; a spread says it is being corrupted rather than misunderstood.
Public RxWhyCount(14) As Long
Public RxWhyName(14) As String * 30        'labels, filled in BeginProg

' Line shape over the whole capture.
Public RxLenMin                    'shortest and longest complete line seen. The
Public RxLenMax                    'two being equal means fixed-length records
Public RxTermCR                    'lines ended by a bare CR
Public RxTermLF                    'lines ended by LF -- CRLF counts here, and
                                   'the CR before it is stripped as whitespace
Public RxFirstLine As String * 250  'the FIRST line ever seen, kept and never
Public RxFirstLineHex As String * 400 'overwritten. Startup banners and mode
                                   'strings appear once and are gone from the
                                   'last-line display within a second

'--- the raw capture file ----------------------------------------------------
Public CaptureNow As Boolean       'true = append every read to CAPTURE_FILE.
                                   'Set false to stop early; the file stays
Public CaptureReset As Boolean     'set true to erase the file and start again.
                                   'It clears itself once done
Public CaptureBytes As Long        'bytes written so far, against CAPTURE_MAX
Public CaptureState As String * 60 'plain English: capturing, full, stopped, or
                                   'the reason it cannot write

'--- is the queue draining ----------------------------------------------------
'
' One ring buffer, two readers. QDepthHttp and QDepthMqtt are how far behind
' each path is; QDepth is the greater of the two, which is what the buffer
' actually still has to hold and therefore the only one of the three that
' QDropped can be reasoned about from.
'
' The pair being far apart is the diagnostic: it says one path is down and which
' one, at a glance, before anything else is read.
Public QDepth                      'readings the buffer is still holding for at
                                   'least one of the two paths
Public QDepthHttp                  'readings waiting to be POSTed
Public QDepthMqtt                  'readings waiting to be published
Public QPeak                       'the deepest it has ever been, which is what
                                   'says how much outage Q_SIZE actually buys
Public QDropped                    'readings lost to a full queue. Non-zero means
                                   'a link has been down longer than the buffer
                                   'holds -- and, with the two depths above, says
                                   'which link that was

'--- did the last POST work ---------------------------------------------------
Public PostState As String * 40    'plain English: what the poster is doing, or
                                   'why it is not
Public TokenOK As Boolean          'the token file was found and was not empty
Public TokenLen As Long            'how many characters it held -- enough to spot
                                   'a truncated paste without printing the secret
Public PostAttempts
Public PostOK
Public PostFail
Public ConsecFail                  'consecutive failures -- resets on success
Public Backoff                     'seconds until the next attempt, when failing
Public SecsSincePost               'seconds since the last ACCEPTED post, not
                                   'since the last attempt

Public Accepted                    'cumulative, from the endpoint's own answer
Public Duplicates                  'readings it had already stored -- normal after
                                   'a retry, and proof the retry was safe
Public Rejected                    'readings it refused. Non-zero needs reading:
                                   'LastReject says why

Public LastAccepted                'the last POST's answer, on its own
Public LastDuplicates
Public LastRejected
Public LastStatus As Long          'the HTTP status code: 200 good, 401 token,
                                   '400 malformed body
Public LastSocket                  'HTTPPost's own return: >=100 is the TCP
                                   'socket it used, 0 the request never got out,
                                   '-2 the instruction did not execute
Public LastReject As String * 200  'the first "why" out of the last rejected array
Public HTTPResponse As String * 500
Public SentHeader As String * 200  'the request headers as built, TOKEN REDACTED
Public RespHeader As String * 300  'what came back in their place -- see PostBatch
Public LastPayload As String * 200 'the first 200 characters of the last body sent

'--- did the last PUBLISH work ------------------------------------------------
'
' The same questions as the POST block above, asked of the other path, and
' deliberately in the same order and the same words so the two can be read side
' by side. What is missing is the row of accepted/duplicates/rejected counters,
' and that absence is the honest one: MQTT gives a publisher no response channel
' at all. The PUBACK behind MqttResult = 0 is from the BROKER, and means the
' broker has the message -- not that MegaNet has it. Anything more would be this
' program inventing a confirmation nobody sent it.
'
' Where the readings went is answerable, just not from here: the bridge logs
' every message it acks, and meganet.station_health carries what the status
' topic said. docs/ingest-mqtt.md walks through proving it end to end.
Public MqttState As String * 40    'plain English: what the publisher is doing,
                                   'or why it is not
Public MqttTopic As String * 100   'the reading topic this station publishes on,
                                   'built at startup. Read it once at
                                   'commissioning and check it against the ACL
Public MqttStatusTopic As String * 100 'and the status topic, which is also the
                                   'one the Last Will has to be set to
Public MqttAttempts
Public MqttOK
Public MqttFail
Public MqttConsecFail              'consecutive failures -- resets on success
Public MqttBackoff                 'seconds until the next attempt, when failing
Public SecsSinceMqtt               'seconds since the last SUCCESSFUL publish,
                                   'not since the last attempt
Public SecsSinceTry                'and seconds since the last ATTEMPT of any
                                   'kind, which is the one the backoff is
                                   'measured against. The pair diverging is a
                                   'broker being knocked on and not answering
Public MqttPublished               'readings handed to the broker, cumulative.
                                   'NOT readings stored -- see the note above
Public MqttLastBatch               'how many were in the last published message
Public MqttResult As Long          'MQTTPublish's own return: 0 is success and
                                   'anything else is the instruction's error
                                   'code. Non-zero with a plausible topic is
                                   'almost always the broker connection -- the
                                   'MQTT settings, not this program
Public MqttStatusResult As Long    'the same, for the retained status message
Public SecsSinceStatus             'seconds since the status message was last
                                   'published. It is retained, so the broker
                                   'holds it between these
Public MqttStatusBody As String * 100 'what was last published to the status
                                   'topic, verbatim
Public MqttLastPayload As String * 200 'the first 200 characters of the last
                                   'reading message published


'==============================================================================
' 3. WORKING VARIABLES
'==============================================================================

' The unsent-readings ring buffer. QHead is the next slot to write; each path
' has its own next-to-send. A path is caught up when its tail equals QHead, so
' capacity is Q_SIZE - 1 for whichever path is furthest behind.
'
' ONE buffer and two tails rather than two buffers, because the readings are the
' same readings: a CR300 has no memory to hold a second copy of them, and two
' copies would be two things that could disagree about what was heard.
'
' No semaphore guards it, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. The
' main scan owns QHead; the poster sequence owns both tails, and it is a single
' sequence rather than one per path precisely so that stays true -- the two
' halves of it run one after the other and never interleave, so neither tail nor
' the scratch they share can be seen half-updated.
'
' The one crossing is on overflow: QueuePush, in the main scan, drops the oldest
' reading and has to move the tail sitting on it. That was true of the single
' tail before it was true of two, and the comment here used to claim otherwise.
' The window is one instruction wide and needs the buffer to fill in the same
' moment a send completes; what it costs when it is lost is one reading sent
' twice or one dropped a scan early, and a reading sent twice is free by
' contract. A semaphore against that would cost every scan.
Dim qAddr(Q_SIZE) As Long
Dim qVal(Q_SIZE) As Long
Dim qStamp(Q_SIZE) As String * 24
Dim qSus(Q_SIZE) As Boolean
Dim QHead As Long
Dim QTailHttp As Long              'the next slot to POST
Dim QTailMqtt As Long              'the next slot to publish
Dim qLag As Long                   'scratch, for the depth arithmetic in the scan

' Dim, not Public, on purpose: a Public token is readable by anyone who can
' reach the logger, which is the whole thing the token file was for.
Dim IngestToken As String * 80

' One body buffer, shared by both paths. They are built in the same sequence,
' one after the other, and each is handed to its instruction before the next is
' built -- so a second CONTENT_SIZE buffer would be 3 KB of a CR300's memory
' spent holding a string nothing is reading.
Dim ContentsString As String * CONTENT_SIZE
Dim HTTPHeader As String * 300
Dim BatchN As Long                 'readings in the batch currently in flight
Dim bWrap As Boolean               'BuildBatch's two inputs, set by the caller
Dim bFrom As Long                  'immediately before the call: which envelope
                                   'to build, and which tail to build it from
Dim CRLF As String * 2
Dim DQ As String * 1               'a double quote. CHR(34) is the portable way
                                   'to get one into a CRBasic string

' Serial working storage. RxBuf is what SerialInBlock writes into; it is Dim
' rather than Public because the bytes past the count it returned are whatever
' the previous read left there, and a Public variable showing stale tail bytes
' would be a diagnostic that lies. RxBlockHex and RxBlockText are built from
' exactly RxBlockBytes bytes and are the ones to read.
Dim RxBuf As String * 256          'what SerialInBlock writes into
Dim rxByte(BUF_MAX) As Long        'the byte buffer. Numeric, so a NUL is just 0
Dim nBuf As Long
Dim RxLine As String * RX_MAX      'one TEXT line, when there is one
Dim dropN As Long                  'BufDrop
Dim dk2 As Long
Dim fk As Long                     'TakeFrames / DecodeFrame
Dim frSigAt As Long
Dim frEnd As Long                  'FrLenByte and FrTotal are the Publics above.
                                   'They were shadowed here by a lower-case pair
                                   'until CRBasic's case-insensitivity made them
                                   'the same two names declared twice
Dim frLoops As Long
Dim frElemAt As Long
Dim frRec As Long
Dim tk2 As Long                    'TakeTextLine
Dim tb As Long
Dim tEnd As Long
Dim tOK As Boolean
Dim tTook As Boolean               'TakeTextLine consumed something
Dim frDone As Boolean              'TakeFrames: this pass made no progress
Dim frIsAscii As Boolean

Dim fld(FIELD_MAX) As String * 16
Dim nFld As Long
Dim pay(32) As Long                'payload bytes, decoded from hex
Dim nPay As Long
Dim frameOk As Long
Dim payLen As Long
Dim frameSecs As Long
Dim loggerSecs As Long
Dim suspect As Long

Dim rTime(9) As Long               '1 yr, 2 mo, 3 day, 4 hr, 5 min, 6 sec,
                                   '7 usec, 8 day-of-week, 9 day-of-year

' JSON fragments, built once in BeginProg so the per-reading concatenation in
' MakeRec stays legible instead of becoming a wall of CHR(34).
'
' Three of these comments had no apostrophe at all -- the fragment was sitting
' in the source as bare code -- and two more carried an odd number of double
' quotes, which throws CRBasic Editor's syntax colouring off for everything
' after them. So a tilde stands for a double quote below and the comments carry
' no quote characters of their own. Every line here begins with an apostrophe,
' which is the rule for every comment in this file.
Dim jEnvelope As String * 200      '~path~:~..~,~protocol~:~..~,~readings~:[
Dim jTail As String * 8            ']}} -- closes the HTTP payload wrapper too
Dim jTailBare As String * 8        ']} -- the MQTT one, which has no wrapper
Dim fId As String * 16             '{~alert_id~:
Dim fTs As String * 20             ',~reading_ts~:~
Dim fVal As String * 20            '~,~value_raw~:
Dim fSus As String * 24            ',~quality~:~suspect~
Dim jName As String * 24           'BASE_NAME, safe to put inside a JSON literal
Dim nk2 As Long
Dim nc2 As Long
Dim jRec As String * 120           'one reading, being built
Dim numStr As String * 16          'one number, formatted

' Scratch, one set per subroutine, so a call never quietly clobbers its caller's
' loop counter. CRBasic has no local scope worth relying on here.
Dim dk As Long                     'DumpBytes
Dim dc As Long
Dim dumpLen As Long
Dim dumpSrc As String * 256
Dim dumpHex As String * 400        'truncated at DUMP_MAX, for the display
Dim dumpHexFull As String * 800    'never truncated, for the capture file
Dim dumpTxt As String * 200
Dim dumpCensus As Boolean          'true only for the block read, so the byte
                                   'census counts each byte once
Dim ck As Long                     'Census
Dim cc As Long
Dim cj As Long
Dim charTally(126) As Long         'one counter per printable byte value, reset
                                   'per line. 126 entries so byte 126 has a slot;
                                   'nothing below 33 is tallied here because the
                                   'control characters are counted as a class
Dim best As Long                   'PickDelim
Dim bestN As Long
Dim capBuf As String * CAP_BUF     'capture bytes waiting to go to flash
Dim capLine As String * 820        'one capture record, being built
Dim capFh As Long
Dim capWrote As Long
Dim capSecs As Long
Dim whyCode As Long                'set before every Call RejectLine()
Dim hk As Long                     'HexByte
Dim hn As Long
Dim hexVal As Long
Dim hexIn As String * 4
Dim fi As Long                     'FindStr
Dim srcStr As String * 500
Dim needle As String * 32
Dim foundAt As Long
Dim si As Long                     'SplitLine
Dim sStart As Long
Dim sChar As String * 1
Dim delim As String * 1
Dim jp As Long                     'JsonInt
Dim jd As Long
Dim jsonKey As String * 16
Dim jsonVal As Long
Dim pp As Long                     'ParseStatus
Dim pd As Long
Dim qn As Long                     'QueuePush
Dim mi As Long                     'MakeRec -- the queue slot being rendered
Dim tk As Long                     'ParseLine trimming -- main scan only
Dim ltk As Long                    'LoadToken trimming. A separate counter from
                                   'tk on purpose: LoadToken runs in the POSTER
                                   'sequence and ParseLine in the main scan, and
                                   'a shared counter between two tasks is a
                                   'mis-trimmed token that fails to authenticate
                                   'once in a blue moon and never reproduces
Dim ak As Long                     'ParseLine's own search for "ALERT2A". Not
Dim aFound As Long                 'FindStr, for the same reason: FindStr's
                                   'srcStr/needle/foundAt belong to the poster,
                                   'which parses the HTTP response with them
Dim pNum As String * 16            'the poster's number scratch. numStr is the
                                   'main scan's, and DumpBytes writes it once
                                   'per byte -- a JSON value built through the
                                   'same variable would eventually be built out
                                   'of a hex pair from the serial port
Dim dNum As String * 16            'QueuePush's own copy of MakeRec's scratch
Dim numIn As String * 16           'IsNum
Dim numOK As Boolean
Dim nk As Long
Dim nc As Long
Dim fh As Long                     'LoadToken -- the file handle
Dim i As Long                      'ParseAlert2, and nothing else: the scan that
                                   'calls it must not use i for its own counter
Dim bi As Long                     'BuildBatch
Dim postDue As Boolean
Dim mqttWait As Long               'seconds the broker is to be left alone for
Dim mqttSent As Boolean            'this tick's publish actually went out
Dim mNum As String * 16            'the publisher's number scratch. A third one,
                                   'for the same reason pNum is a second: it is
                                   'written while ContentsString is being handed
                                   'to an instruction, and sharing it with the
                                   'poster is a status message eventually built
                                   'out of half a reading
Dim statusDue As Boolean


'==============================================================================
' 4. DATA TABLES
'==============================================================================

' The local archive, and the reason a broken link is an inconvenience rather
' than a data loss. Every reading is stored here whether or not it ever posts,
' so a base station that spent a fortnight offline can still be collected over
' a cable and backfilled.
'
' Sized, not auto-allocated. Campbell costs an auto-allocated event-driven table
' at one record per second, so a -1 here would claim almost all the memory and
' starve the heartbeat below.
DataTable (Readings,True,20000)
  Sample (1,RxLastId,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastValue,Long)
  Sample (1,RxFrameSkew,IEEE4)
EndTable

' One record per serial read that returned bytes: the raw block and the buffer
' state around it. This is the table that makes the rest of them checkable --
' every other row in every other table is derived from these bytes, so if a
' decode looks wrong the question "what actually arrived" is answered here and
' nowhere else. RxBlockHexFull is not truncated.
DataTable (RawLog,True,400)
  Sample (1,RxBlockSeq,Long)
  Sample (1,RxAvail,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBlockBytes,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBufLenBefore,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBufLen,Long)
  Sample (1,RxNulls,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBlockHexFull,String)
EndTable

' One record per binary frame found, INCLUDING the ones that did not decode --
' a frame that yields nothing is the row that says why, and a table holding only
' successes cannot answer the question this table exists for.
'
' FrElemVia is the one to read first when a frame does not produce readings:
' 1 means the 84 01 <len> 74 anchor matched, 2 means only the loose scan did,
' 0 means neither and the frame carries no concentration element at all.
' FrLeftover near zero confirms the 6 + <length byte> rule frame by frame.
DataTable (FrameLog,True,400)
  Sample (1,RxFrameSeq,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBlockSeq,Long)
  Sample (1,FrPre,Long)
  Sample (1,FrLenByte,Long)
  Sample (1,FrTotal,Long)
  Sample (1,FrLeftover,Long)
  Sample (1,FrElemVia,Long)
  Sample (1,FrElemOff,Long)
  Sample (1,FrElemLen,Long)
  Sample (1,FrTimeSecs,Long)
  Sample (1,FrRecords,Long)
  Sample (1,FrGood,Long)
  Sample (1,FrBad,Long)
  Sample (1,whyCode,Long)
  Sample (1,RxFrameSkew,IEEE4)
  Sample (1,FrElemHex,String)
  Sample (1,FrHex,String)
EndTable

' One record per four-byte reading record, decoded or not. The four bytes are
' here as numbers as well as hex so the bit-unpacking can be recomputed from the
' table by hand: id = (B1 MOD 32) * 256 + B0, value = INT(B1 / 32) * 256 + B2.
DataTable (ReadingLog,True,1000)
  Sample (1,RxFrameSeq,Long)
  Sample (1,RdIndex,Long)
  Sample (1,RdB0,Long)
  Sample (1,RdB1,Long)
  Sample (1,RdB2,Long)
  Sample (1,RdB3,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastId,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastValue,Long)
  Sample (1,RdQueued,Boolean)
  Sample (1,RdRecHex,String)
EndTable

' One record per complete line, whether it parsed or not.
'
' The first version of this table was numbers only, to keep it cheap enough to
' leave running for hours. That was the wrong trade and a real capture proved
' it: a table of lengths and field counts establishes that a feed HAS a
' consistent shape without ever saying what the shape IS, so the table narrowed
' the question and could not answer it. Two short text samples cost about 56
' bytes a record and settle it, so they are here and the record count is lower.
'
' The census columns are here for the same reason. RxLastDelimCode only reports
' the five separators this program tests for; RxTopSepDec reports the most
' common non-alphanumeric byte in the line whatever it is, which is what
' identifies a feed delimited by something nobody thought to test.
'
' delim  0 none  1 comma  2 semicolon  3 tab  4 pipe  5 space
' shape  1 ALERT2A  2 plain  3 unrecognised
' why    0 accepted, otherwise the RxWhyName() index
DataTable (LineLog,True,2000)
  Sample (1,RxLastLineLen,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastFields,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastDelimCode,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastShapeCode,Long)
  Sample (1,whyCode,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastPrefix,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepComma,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepSpace,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepColon,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepEq,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepHi,Long)
  Sample (1,RxSepCtl,Long)
  Sample (1,RxTopSepDec,Long)
  Sample (1,RxTopSepN,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLastHead,String)
  Sample (1,RxLastTail,String)
EndTable

' The heartbeat. Five minutes is frequent enough to see a link flapping and slow
' enough to keep months on a CR300. It carries BOTH paths' results as well as the
' counters, which is what answers "when did it stop working, and which half of
' it" -- there is no separate per-attempt log table and no second table for the
' publisher, on purpose: one table read in one place beats two that have to be
' lined up against each other, and lining the two paths up against each other is
' the entire question this table now exists to answer.
'
' Counters are Long, not FP2: FP2 carries four significant digits and cannot
' hold an integer above 7999 exactly, so a POST counter stored as FP2 starts
' lying on about the fourth day.
DataTable (Diag,True,-1)
  DataInterval (0,5,Min,10)
  Minimum (1,Batt_volt,FP2,False,False)
  Sample (1,PTemp,FP2)
  Sample (1,WatchdogErrs,Long)
  Sample (1,SkippedScans,Long)
  Sample (1,VarOutOfBound,Long)
  Sample (1,NTPErrMs,FP2)
  Sample (1,RxBytesTotal,Long)
  Sample (1,RxNulls,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLines,Long)
  Sample (1,RxFrames,Long)
  Sample (1,RxReadings,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBadFrames,Long)
  Sample (1,SecsSinceRx,Long)
  Sample (1,QDepth,Long)
  Sample (1,QDepthHttp,Long)
  Sample (1,QDepthMqtt,Long)
  Sample (1,QPeak,Long)
  Sample (1,QDropped,Long)
  Sample (1,Accepted,Long)
  Sample (1,Duplicates,Long)
  Sample (1,Rejected,Long)
  Sample (1,PostOK,Long)
  Sample (1,PostFail,Long)
  Sample (1,LastStatus,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttOK,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttFail,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttPublished,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttResult,Long)
  Sample (1,PostState,String)
  Sample (1,MqttState,String)
  Sample (1,ClockState,String)
  Sample (1,LastReject,String)
EndTable


'==============================================================================
' 5. SUBROUTINES
'==============================================================================
'
' Declared before BeginProg, and in call order. They write their answers into
' module-level variables rather than returning them: it reads a little flatter
' than a Function would, and it sidesteps every question about how CRBasic
' passes strings and arrays into a call.

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' DumpBytes -- dumpLen bytes of dumpSrc, three ways at once:
'
'   dumpHex     padded hex, truncated at DUMP_MAX, for the display
'   dumpHexFull the same and never truncated, for RawLog and the capture file
'   dumpTxt     printable, with control characters shown as a dash
'
' This is the KDO driver's diagnostic loop, and it is the reason this program's
' serial handling can be checked rather than guessed at. Every byte goes through
' ASCII(), so what is printed is what arrived -- not what a string function
' decided the string was.
'
' The walk is bounded by dumpLen, the count the caller got back from
' SerialInBlock, and not by Len(dumpSrc). Len() stops at the first NUL, and the
' port is open in transparent mode where a NUL is an ordinary byte.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub DumpBytes()
  dumpHex = ""
  dumpHexFull = ""
  dumpTxt = ""
  For dk = 1 To dumpLen
    dc = ASCII(dumpSrc(1,1,dk))

    'Counted, never removed. An earlier version dropped NUL bytes here so that
    'the result could live in a CRBasic string; the frames this receiver sends
    'carry nine to eleven of them, so that one line destroyed every frame.
    If dc = 0 AND dumpCensus Then RxNulls = RxNulls + 1

    Sprintf (numStr,"%02X ",dc)
    dumpHexFull = dumpHexFull & numStr

    If dk <= DUMP_MAX Then
      dumpHex = dumpHex & numStr
      If dc < 32 OR dc > 126 Then
        dumpTxt = dumpTxt & "-"
      Else
        dumpTxt = dumpTxt & CHR(dc)
      EndIf
    EndIf

    'The byte census, on the block read only -- this sub is also called for the
    'assembled line and for the buffer contents, and counting those too would
    'count most bytes three times.
    If dumpCensus Then
      If dc = 0 Then
        RxByteClass(1) = RxByteClass(1) + 1
      ElseIf dc = 13 Then
        RxByteClass(3) = RxByteClass(3) + 1
      ElseIf dc = 10 Then
        RxByteClass(4) = RxByteClass(4) + 1
      ElseIf dc = 9 Then
        RxByteClass(5) = RxByteClass(5) + 1
      ElseIf dc = 32 Then
        RxByteClass(6) = RxByteClass(6) + 1
      ElseIf dc < 32 Then
        RxByteClass(2) = RxByteClass(2) + 1
      ElseIf dc < 127 Then
        RxByteClass(7) = RxByteClass(7) + 1
      Else
        RxByteClass(8) = RxByteClass(8) + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next dk

  If dumpLen > DUMP_MAX Then
    dumpHex = dumpHex & "..."
    dumpTxt = dumpTxt & "..."
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Census -- count the separator characters in RxLine, into RxLastSep.
'
' The single most useful line on the whole Public table when a feed will not
' parse, because "unrecognised line" only ever means one thing: the splitter
' found fewer than two fields, and this says why. Every count zero and a long
' RxLastLineLen is a line that is one unbroken token. A non-zero `hi` is a feed
' that is not ASCII, and nothing downstream of here can help with that.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub Census()
  RxSepComma = 0
  RxSepSpace = 0
  RxSepTab = 0
  RxSepSemi = 0
  RxSepColon = 0
  RxSepPipe = 0
  RxSepEq = 0
  RxSepHi = 0
  RxSepCtl = 0
  RxLastHead = ""
  RxLastTail = ""
  For ck = 33 To 126
    charTally(ck) = 0
  Next ck

  For ck = 1 To Len(RxLine)
    cc = ASCII(Mid(RxLine,ck,1))

    If cc = 44 Then RxSepComma = RxSepComma + 1
    If cc = 32 Then RxSepSpace = RxSepSpace + 1
    If cc = 9 Then RxSepTab = RxSepTab + 1
    If cc = 59 Then RxSepSemi = RxSepSemi + 1
    If cc = 58 Then RxSepColon = RxSepColon + 1
    If cc = 124 Then RxSepPipe = RxSepPipe + 1
    If cc = 61 Then RxSepEq = RxSepEq + 1
    If cc > 126 Then RxSepHi = RxSepHi + 1
    If cc < 32 AND cc <> 9 Then RxSepCtl = RxSepCtl + 1

    If cc >= 33 AND cc <= 126 Then charTally(cc) = charTally(cc) + 1

    'The two samples, from the same pass. Anything unprintable becomes a dot so
    'the sample cannot scramble a table export.
    If cc < 32 OR cc > 126 Then cc = 46
    If ck <= 40 Then RxLastHead = RxLastHead & CHR(cc)
    If ck > Len(RxLine) - 16 Then RxLastTail = RxLastTail & CHR(cc)
  Next ck

  'The most common character that is neither a letter nor a digit. Whatever a
  'feed is delimited by, this finds it without having been told to look.
  RxTopSepDec = 0
  RxTopSepN = 0
  RxTopSep = ""
  For cj = 33 To 126
    If (cj >= 48 AND cj <= 57) OR (cj >= 65 AND cj <= 90) OR (cj >= 97 AND cj <= 122) Then
      'a letter or a digit, so not a separator
    ElseIf charTally(cj) > RxTopSepN Then
      RxTopSepN = charTally(cj)
      RxTopSepDec = cj
    EndIf
  Next cj
  If RxTopSepDec > 0 Then RxTopSep = CHR(RxTopSepDec)

  'Built in several calls rather than one wide one. Sprintf's argument limit is
  'not something this program can check, and a format string that quietly drops
  'its tail would make the most-read diagnostic on the table the least
  'trustworthy one.
  Sprintf (numStr,"comma=%d ",RxSepComma)
  RxLastSep = numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"space=%d ",RxSepSpace)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"tab=%d ",RxSepTab)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"semi=%d ",RxSepSemi)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"colon=%d ",RxSepColon)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"pipe=%d ",RxSepPipe)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"eq=%d ",RxSepEq)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"hi=%d ",RxSepHi)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"ctl=%d ",RxSepCtl)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
  Sprintf (numStr,"top=%d x%d",RxTopSepDec,RxTopSepN)
  RxLastSep = RxLastSep & numStr
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PickDelim -- choose the separator to split RxLine on, from what Census counted.
'
' Comma first and replaced only on a strictly greater count, so an ALERT2 line
' still splits on comma even if it happens to contain as many spaces. "none"
' leaves delim at a character that cannot appear, which yields one field and the
' honest "unrecognised line" rather than a plausible wrong parse.
'
' The first version of this program tried comma, then space, and nothing else --
' which is why a tab or semicolon separated feed came out as "unrecognised" with
' no clue as to what it actually was.
'
' "none" means the caller does not split at all. An earlier draft set delim to a
' character assumed not to occur; a binary feed containing that byte then split
' into two fields and reached ParsePlain, which is the one outcome worse than
' "unrecognised" -- a plausible reading for a station that never sent one.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub PickDelim()
  best = 0
  bestN = 0
  If RxSepComma > bestN Then
    bestN = RxSepComma
    best = 1
  EndIf
  If RxSepSemi > bestN Then
    bestN = RxSepSemi
    best = 2
  EndIf
  If RxSepTab > bestN Then
    bestN = RxSepTab
    best = 3
  EndIf
  If RxSepPipe > bestN Then
    bestN = RxSepPipe
    best = 4
  EndIf
  If RxSepSpace > bestN Then
    bestN = RxSepSpace
    best = 5
  EndIf

  RxLastDelimCode = best
  If best = 1 Then
    delim = ","
    RxLastDelim = "comma"
  ElseIf best = 2 Then
    delim = ";"
    RxLastDelim = "semicolon"
  ElseIf best = 3 Then
    delim = CHR(9)
    RxLastDelim = "tab"
  ElseIf best = 4 Then
    delim = "|"
    RxLastDelim = "pipe"
  ElseIf best = 5 Then
    delim = " "
    RxLastDelim = "space"
  Else
    delim = ""
    RxLastDelim = "none"
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' CapFlush -- the capture buffer to flash, and nothing if it is empty.
'
' Open, append, close. Holding the handle open across scans would lose the whole
' file to a power cycle, which is the one thing a capture must not do.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub CapFlush()
  capSecs = 0
  If Len(capBuf) = 0 Then ExitSub
  capFh = FileOpen (CAPTURE_FILE,"a",0)
  If capFh <> 0 Then
    capWrote = FileWrite (capFh,capBuf,Len(capBuf))
    FileClose (capFh)
    CaptureBytes = CaptureBytes + capWrote
    capBuf = ""
  Else
    CaptureState = "cannot write " & CAPTURE_FILE
    capBuf = ""                                  'drop it rather than wedge
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' CapStart -- erase the capture file and write its header.
'
' Mode "w" truncates, so there is no delete to get wrong. The header records the
' port settings the capture was taken at, because a capture of the wrong baud
' rate looks exactly like a capture of a broken sensor.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub CapStart()
  capBuf = ""
  CaptureBytes = 0
  capFh = FileOpen (CAPTURE_FILE,"w",0)
  If capFh <> 0 Then
    Sprintf (capLine,"# MegaNet base-station serial capture -- baud %d",RX_BAUD)
    Sprintf (numStr," format %d",RX_FORMAT)
    capLine = capLine & numStr & CRLF
    capLine = capLine & "# started " & NowISO & " (logger clock, UTC)" & CRLF
    capLine = capLine & "# one record per read: hh:mm:ss n=<bytes> <hex>" & CRLF
    capWrote = FileWrite (capFh,capLine,Len(capLine))
    FileClose (capFh)
    CaptureBytes = capWrote
    CaptureState = "capturing"
  Else
    CaptureState = "cannot create " & CAPTURE_FILE
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' CaptureBlock -- this scan's bytes into the capture buffer, verbatim.
'
' dumpHexFull, not dumpHex: the display copy stops at DUMP_MAX and a truncated
' capture is worse than none, because it looks complete. Nothing here is parsed,
' filtered or interpreted -- that is the point of it.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub CaptureBlock()
  If NOT CaptureNow Then
    CaptureState = "stopped - set CaptureNow to resume"
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If CaptureBytes >= CAPTURE_MAX Then
    CaptureState = "full - retrieve " & CAPTURE_FILE
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  If ClockOK Then
    capLine = Mid(NowISO,12,8)
  Else
    capLine = "--:--:--"
  EndIf
  Sprintf (numStr," n=%d ",RxBlockBytes)
  capLine = capLine & numStr & dumpHexFull & CRLF

  If Len(capBuf) + Len(capLine) > CAP_BUF - 4 Then Call CapFlush()
  capBuf = capBuf & capLine
  CaptureState = "capturing"
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' HexByte -- hex characters in hexIn, the value out in hexVal, -1 if not hex.
'
' Done from ASCII codes rather than with a conversion function so it compiles on
' every operating system that has HTTPPost at all, and so a stray character in a
' line a terminal wrapped is a counted rejection rather than a silent zero.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub HexByte()
  hexVal = 0
  If Len(hexIn) = 0 Then
    hexVal = -1
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  For hk = 1 To Len(hexIn)
    hn = ASCII(Mid(hexIn,hk,1))
    If hn >= 48 AND hn <= 57 Then
      hn = hn - 48                               '0-9
    ElseIf hn >= 65 AND hn <= 70 Then
      hn = hn - 55                               'A-F
    ElseIf hn >= 97 AND hn <= 102 Then
      hn = hn - 87                               'a-f
    Else
      hexVal = -1
      ExitSub
    EndIf
    hexVal = hexVal * 16 + hn
  Next hk
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' FindStr -- the position of `needle` in `srcStr`, into foundAt. 0 if absent.
'
' This is InStr, written out. See the note at the top of the file for why: the
' option code InStr takes could not be verified, and the failure mode of a wrong
' one is a silent mis-parse rather than a compile error.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub FindStr()
  foundAt = 0
  If Len(needle) = 0 Then ExitSub
  If Len(srcStr) < Len(needle) Then ExitSub
  For fi = 1 To Len(srcStr) - Len(needle) + 1
    If Mid(srcStr,fi,Len(needle)) = needle Then
      foundAt = fi
      ExitFor
    EndIf
  Next fi
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' SplitLine -- RxLine split on `delim` into fld(), count into nFld.
'
' This is SplitStr, written out, for the same reason. Fields past FIELD_MAX are
' counted but not stored, so a runaway line cannot write past the array -- and
' CRBasic does not bounds-check a variable array index, so that guard is the
' only thing between a malformed line and memory that belongs to something else.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub SplitLine()
  nFld = 0
  sStart = 1
  For si = 1 To Len(RxLine) + 1
    If si > Len(RxLine) Then
      sChar = delim                              'flush the final field
    Else
      sChar = Mid(RxLine,si,1)
    EndIf
    If sChar = delim Then
      nFld = nFld + 1
      If nFld <= FIELD_MAX Then fld(nFld) = Mid(RxLine,sStart,si - sStart)
      sStart = si + 1
    EndIf
  Next si
  If nFld > FIELD_MAX Then nFld = FIELD_MAX
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' IsNum -- is numIn a plain integer? Answer in numOK.
'
' An optional leading minus and then digits, nothing else. ParsePlain needs this
' because CRBasic converts a string to a number on assignment and gives 0 for
' anything it cannot read -- so ":HS=1" becomes alert id 0 silently, and a line
' from an entirely different protocol turns into a reading rather than into a
' rejection anyone would notice.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub IsNum()
  numOK = False
  If Len(numIn) = 0 Then ExitSub
  For nk = 1 To Len(numIn)
    nc = ASCII(Mid(numIn,nk,1))
    If nk = 1 AND nc = 45 AND Len(numIn) > 1 Then
      'a leading minus is allowed
    ElseIf nc < 48 OR nc > 57 Then
      ExitSub
    EndIf
  Next nk
  numOK = True
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' StampNow -- the clock, as "2026-08-11T04:15:00Z", into NowISO.
'
' The logger's clock is UTC (NTP offset 0, above), so the Z is the truth and not
' a claim. ClockOK is set here and nowhere else: a reading is only queued when it
' is true, because the endpoint's most common rejection is a reading stamped
' 1970 by an RTC that lost its backup battery, and a base station that posts a
' thousand of those has made a battery fault look like a network fault.
'
' Sprintf rather than FormatFloat because the zero-padded widths are the whole
' point and Sprintf takes integer specifiers natively -- RealTime fills a Long
' array, so %d matches its arguments exactly.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub StampNow()
  RealTime (rTime())
  If rTime(1) < MIN_YEAR Then
    ClockOK = False
    ClockState = "clock is before " & MIN_YEAR & " - check the RTC battery"
    NowISO = ""
  ElseIf REQUIRE_NTP AND Len(NTP_SERVER) > 0 AND NOT ClockVerified Then
    'The year is plausible, which proves nothing about the zone. See REQUIRE_NTP.
    ClockOK = False
    ClockState = "waiting for the first NTP sync - not stamping yet"
    NowISO = ""
  Else
    ClockOK = True
    ClockState = "ok"
    Sprintf (NowISO,"%04d-%02d-%02dT%02d:%02d:%02dZ",rTime(1),rTime(2),rTime(3),rTime(4),rTime(5),rTime(6))
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' MakeRec -- queue slot `mi` rendered as one JSON object, into jRec.
'
' Called only from BuildBatch, which is to say only from the poster sequence.
' QueuePush renders the same object for RxLastJson with its own copy of these
' six lines rather than calling this, because the two run in different tasks and
' sharing jRec, mi and a scratch string between them would mean a POST body
' occasionally built out of a reading the main scan was mid-way through
' displaying. **The two renderings must be kept identical** -- that is a cost of
' the split, and a cheaper one than a wrong value_raw nobody can reproduce.
'
' Numbers go through Sprintf rather than being concatenated directly. Implicit
' numeric-to-string conversion is free to pad, round or reach for scientific
' notation, and any of the three inside a JSON literal is a rejected batch.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub MakeRec()
  Sprintf (pNum,"%d",qAddr(mi))
  jRec = fId & pNum
  jRec = jRec & fTs & qStamp(mi)
  Sprintf (pNum,"%d",qVal(mi))
  jRec = jRec & fVal & pNum
  If qSus(mi) Then jRec = jRec & fSus
  jRec = jRec & "}"
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' QueuePush -- one reading into the ring buffer. Address in RxLastId, value in
' RxLastValue, suspect flag in `suspect`.
'
' Called from the main scan, which is the only writer of QHead. The poster
' sequence advances both tails as it delivers; this is the one place outside it
' that touches them, and only when the buffer is full. See the note at the
' declaration for why that crossing is left unguarded.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub QueuePush()
  qn = QHead + 1
  If qn > Q_SIZE Then qn = 1
  If qn = QTailHttp OR qn = QTailMqtt Then
    'Full for whichever path is furthest behind. Drop the oldest rather than
    'refuse the newest -- at a flood warning base the current river level is the
    'reading that matters.
    '
    'Every tail sitting on the dropped slot moves, and only those: if HTTP has
    'already sent this reading and MQTT has not, HTTP's tail is somewhere ahead
    'and has no business moving. A tail left pointing at a slot the scan has
    'just overwritten is the one bug this arrangement can produce, and it is a
    'silent one -- that path would wake up and send a reading nobody heard.
    If qn = QTailHttp Then
      QTailHttp = QTailHttp + 1
      If QTailHttp > Q_SIZE Then QTailHttp = 1
    EndIf
    If qn = QTailMqtt Then
      QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt + 1
      If QTailMqtt > Q_SIZE Then QTailMqtt = 1
    EndIf
    QDropped = QDropped + 1
  EndIf
  qAddr(QHead) = RxLastId
  qVal(QHead) = RxLastValue
  qStamp(QHead) = NowISO
  qSus(QHead) = (suspect = 1)

  'Step 6 and step 7 of the pipeline display, taken from the slot just written.
  'These six lines are MakeRec's, deliberately copied rather than called -- see
  'the note there. If you change one, change both.
  RxLastSuspect = (suspect = 1)
  RxLastStamp = NowISO
  Sprintf (dNum,"%d",RxLastId)
  RxLastJson = fId & dNum
  RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fTs & NowISO
  Sprintf (dNum,"%d",RxLastValue)
  RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fVal & dNum
  If RxLastSuspect Then RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fSus
  RxLastJson = RxLastJson & "}"
  RxStep = "7 queued"

  QHead = qn
  RxReadings = RxReadings + 1
  CallTable Readings
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' RejectLine -- one place to record a line that would not decode, so every
' rejection leaves the same evidence behind. Reason in RxLastWhy and its number
' in whyCode before the call.
'
' The hex is kept as well as the text, because a line that will not parse is
' quite often a line whose text is not readable -- and RxWhyCount() is kept
' because "which rejection, how many times" is the question a capture is run to
' answer, and a last-line snapshot cannot answer it.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub RejectLine()
  RxLastBad = RxLine
  RxLastBadHex = RxLastLineHex
  RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
  If whyCode >= 1 AND whyCode <= 14 Then RxWhyCount(whyCode) = RxWhyCount(whyCode) + 1
  RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ParsePlain -- "<alert id>,<value>" or "<alert id> <value>".
'
' The fallback for any decoder that is not an ERT-A2, and what you type into a
' terminal to prove the whole path end to end without waiting for a
' transmission. logger/README.md walks through that test.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ParsePlain()
  RxLastRecHex = ""
  RxLastPayLen = 0
  RxLastPayHex = ""

  'Both fields must be numbers before either is read as one. See IsNum.
  numIn = fld(1)
  Call IsNum()
  If numOK Then
    numIn = fld(2)
    Call IsNum()
  EndIf
  If NOT numOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "fields are not numbers - not this protocol"
    whyCode = 12
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxLastId = fld(1)                              'CRBasic converts the text on
  RxLastValue = fld(2)                           'assignment to a numeric

  If RxLastId < 1 OR RxLastId > 65535 Then
    RxLastWhy = "alert id outside 1-65535"
    whyCode = 9
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  Call StampNow()
  If NOT ClockOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
    whyCode = 8
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1
  RxFrameSkew = NAN                              'no frame clock in this shape
  RxStep = "6 reading decoded"
  suspect = 0
  If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
  Call QueuePush()
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ParseAlert2 -- the ERT-A2's ALERT2 ASCII protocol.
'
'   ALERT2A,1,9999,ELPRO,N,1,2026,6,8,19,10,41.296,0,0,0,0,0,1,0,0,0,7,7,9999,74,64,F0,7E,18,15,00
'   |_______________________ 24 fixed fields ________________________|__ payload __|
'
' Field 18 is the frame-valid flag, field 23 the payload length in bytes, and
' field 24 the transmitting node. The full table, and the evidence behind each
' entry, is in alert2.js -- this program reads the three fields that decide
' whether a frame is worth anything and leaves the rest to the app.
'
' The payload is an ALERT2 "ALERT concentration" element:
'
'   byte 0     0x74, the element type
'   bytes 1-2  seconds since midnight, from the transmitting network
'   then four bytes per reading:
'     byte 0   AAAAAAAA   address bits 7-0
'     byte 1   DDDAAAAA   value bits 10-8, then address bits 12-8
'     byte 2   DDDDDDDD   value bits 7-0
'     byte 3   SSSSSSSS   status; 0 on every valid record observed
'
' That second byte is the only part of the encoding that is not obvious by eye:
' a byte that looks like address is carrying the top of the value too. It is
' split with MOD and INT rather than with shift operators -- CRBasic has none,
' and the arithmetic is exact on integers this small:
'
'   address high bits   pay(i+1) MOD 32       the low five bits
'   value   high bits   INT(pay(i+1) / 32)    the top three
'
' RxLastRecHex shows the four bytes each reading came out of, so this can be
' checked by hand on the bench against the table above.
'
' Nothing is decoded on a best-effort basis. A line that is not understood in
' full is rejected whole, because half an ALERT frame is a plausible-looking
' reading for the wrong station, and a wrong station is worse than a gap.
'
' --- Which clock stamps the reading -----------------------------------------
'
' This one, not either of the two in the frame. The ERT-A2's own RTC is in
' fields 7-12 and is demonstrably unreliable -- the 444-frame reference capture
' behind alert2.js has it twelve hours out, an AM/PM error on the unit. The
' payload's seconds-since-midnight comes from the transmitting network and is
' the better of the two, but nothing in the frame or in ELPRO's documentation
' says which zone it counts from, and a base station that guessed wrong would
' silently shift every reading it ever posted by a whole number of hours.
'
' So the reading is stamped with this logger's NTP-disciplined UTC clock at the
' moment the line arrives -- receive latency at a base station is seconds -- and
' the disagreement is reported rather than resolved. RxFrameSkew is the frame
' time minus this logger's time of day: near zero means the two agree and the
' frame time is UTC; parked at a whole number of hours is the answer to the
' question above, and worth telling MegaNet about.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ParseAlert2()
  RxLastPayLen = 0
  RxLastPayHex = ""
  RxLastRecHex = ""

  If nFld < N_FIELDS + 4 Then
    RxLastWhy = "short line - wrapped or truncated"
    whyCode = 1
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Field 18 was 1 on all 443 good frames in the reference capture and 0 on the
  'single corrupt one, whose records also carried addresses matching no station.
  'So this is a gate rather than a warning.
  frameOk = fld(18)
  If frameOk <> 1 Then
    RxLastWhy = "receiver flagged the frame invalid"
    whyCode = 2
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Field 23 is the payload length in bytes, and it matched the number of
  'trailing hex fields on all 444 frames -- so it is what tells a complete line
  'from one a narrow terminal wrapped.
  payLen = fld(23)
  nPay = nFld - N_FIELDS
  RxLastPayLen = payLen
  If payLen <> nPay Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload length disagrees with the hex"
    whyCode = 3
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If nPay < 7 OR nPay > 32 Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload too short or too long"
    whyCode = 4
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If ((nPay - 3) MOD 4) <> 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload is not whole records"
    whyCode = 5
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Hex to bytes. pay(1) is payload byte 0 -- CRBasic arrays start at 1. The
  'result is echoed into RxLastPayHex so the conversion can be compared against
  'the tail of RxLastLineHex without any arithmetic.
  For i = 1 To nPay
    hexIn = fld(N_FIELDS + i)
    Call HexByte()
    If hexVal < 0 OR hexVal > 255 Then
      RxLastWhy = "payload byte is not hex"
      whyCode = 6
      Call RejectLine()
      ExitSub
    EndIf
    pay(i) = hexVal
    Sprintf (numStr,"%02X ",hexVal)
    RxLastPayHex = RxLastPayHex & numStr
  Next i
  RxStep = "5 payload decoded"

  If pay(1) <> ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
    RxLastWhy = "not an ALERT concentration element"
    whyCode = 7
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  Call StampNow()
  If NOT ClockOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
    whyCode = 8
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1

  'The frame's own clock, against ours. See the note above.
  frameSecs = pay(2) * 256 + pay(3)
  loggerSecs = rTime(4) * 3600 + rTime(5) * 60 + rTime(6)
  RxFrameSkew = frameSecs - loggerSecs
  If RxFrameSkew > 43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew - 86400
  If RxFrameSkew < -43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew + 86400

  For i = 4 To nPay Step 4
    Sprintf (RxLastRecHex,"%02X %02X %02X %02X",pay(i),pay(i + 1),pay(i + 2),pay(i + 3))
    If pay(i + 3) <> 0 Then
      'A non-zero status byte marked corrupt records in the reference capture,
      'and those records carried addresses matching no station. Counted, not
      'posted.
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    Else
      RxLastId = (pay(i + 1) MOD 32) * 256 + pay(i)
      RxLastValue = INT(pay(i + 1) / 32) * 256 + pay(i + 2)
      RxStep = "6 reading decoded"
      If RxLastId >= 1 AND RxLastId <= 65535 Then
        suspect = 0
        If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
        Call QueuePush()
      Else
        RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next i
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ParseLine -- one complete line in RxLine, zero or more readings into the queue.
'
' Two shapes, told apart by what the line starts with, the same way alert2.js
' sniffs a capture. Sniffing on the raw text rather than on a split field means
' a line whose delimiters are wrong is still identified before it is blamed.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ParseLine()
  RxLastWhy = ""
  RxLastShape = ""
  RxLastPrefix = 0
  whyCode = 0

  'Trim any trailing whitespace the terminator left behind, so the last field is
  'a clean number whichever of CR, LF or CRLF the receiver ends its lines with.
  Do While Len(RxLine) > 0
    tk = ASCII(Mid(RxLine,Len(RxLine),1))
    If tk = 13 OR tk = 10 OR tk = 32 OR tk = 9 Then
      RxLine = Mid(RxLine,1,Len(RxLine) - 1)
    Else
      ExitDo
    EndIf
  Loop
  If Len(RxLine) = 0 Then ExitSub

  RxLines = RxLines + 1
  RxLastLine = RxLine
  RxLastLineLen = Len(RxLine)
  If RxLenMin = 0 OR RxLastLineLen < RxLenMin Then RxLenMin = RxLastLineLen
  If RxLastLineLen > RxLenMax Then RxLenMax = RxLastLineLen

  'The line, in hex, beside the line as text. Same loop as the block dump, so
  'the two displays mean the same thing. The census is off here -- these bytes
  'were counted when the block they arrived in was read.
  dumpSrc = RxLine
  dumpLen = Len(RxLine)
  dumpCensus = False
  Call DumpBytes()
  RxLastLineHex = dumpHex

  'The first line ever seen, kept for good. A receiver's startup banner or mode
  'string appears once and is gone from the last-line display a second later,
  'and it is often the line that says what the rest of the feed is.
  If RxLines = 1 Then
    RxFirstLine = RxLine
    RxFirstLineHex = dumpHex
  EndIf

  'What separators the line actually contains, before anything is assumed about
  'them, and then the one to split on.
  Call Census()
  Call PickDelim()
  RxStep = "4 line captured, delim " & RxLastDelim

  If RxLastDelimCode = 0 Then
    nFld = 1                                     'nothing to split on. See the
  Else                                           'note at PickDelim
    Call SplitLine()
  EndIf
  RxLastFields = nFld

  'ALERT2A found anywhere in the line, not only at the front. A receiver that
  'prefixes its output -- a prompt, a sequence number, a stray byte from the
  'previous frame -- would otherwise be unrecognisable, and RxLastPrefix says
  'how many bytes were stepped over so the prefix itself is not lost.
  aFound = 0
  If Len(RxLine) >= 7 Then
    For ak = 1 To Len(RxLine) - 6
      If Mid(RxLine,ak,7) = "ALERT2A" Then
        aFound = ak
        ExitFor
      EndIf
    Next ak
  EndIf
  If aFound > 0 Then
    If aFound > 1 Then
      RxLastPrefix = aFound - 1
      RxLine = Mid(RxLine,aFound,Len(RxLine) - aFound + 1)
      If RxLastDelimCode > 0 Then Call SplitLine()
      RxLastFields = nFld
    EndIf
    RxLastShape = "ALERT2A"
    RxLastShapeCode = 1
    Call ParseAlert2()
  ElseIf nFld >= 2 Then
    RxLastShape = "plain"
    RxLastShapeCode = 2
    Call ParsePlain()
  Else
    RxLastShape = "unrecognised"
    RxLastShapeCode = 3
    RxLastWhy = "unrecognised line"
    whyCode = 10
    Call RejectLine()
  EndIf

  CallTable LineLog
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' BufDrop -- remove the first dropN bytes from the byte buffer.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub BufDrop()
  If dropN >= nBuf Then
    nBuf = 0
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  For dk2 = 1 To nBuf - dropN
    rxByte(dk2) = rxByte(dk2 + dropN)
  Next dk2
  nBuf = nBuf - dropN
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TakeTextLine -- one printable, CR/LF-terminated run out of the byte buffer.
'
' Only runs that are ENTIRELY printable qualify. That guard is the lesson of the
' first capture: on a binary feed some byte is eventually 0x0D, and treating it
' as the end of a line turns one good frame into two lines of nonsense and two
' rejections that describe nothing. A run that reaches a terminator carrying
' bytes no text line can hold is counted at RxNonText and dropped, so a binary
' frame this program failed to frame is reported as binary rather than as a
' malformed line.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub TakeTextLine()
  tTook = False
  tEnd = 0
  tOK = True
  For tk2 = 1 To nBuf
    tb = rxByte(tk2)
    If tb = 13 OR tb = 10 Then
      tEnd = tk2
      If tb = 13 Then
        RxTermCR = RxTermCR + 1
      Else
        RxTermLF = RxTermLF + 1
      EndIf
      ExitFor
    EndIf
    If tb <> 9 AND (tb < 32 OR tb > 126) Then tOK = False
  Next tk2

  If tEnd = 0 Then
    'Nothing complete in there. Guard the buffer so a feed that never presents
    'anything framable cannot wedge it.
    If nBuf > BUF_MAX - RX_BLOCK Then
      RxBufDrops = RxBufDrops + 1
      dropN = nBuf
      Call BufDrop()
      tTook = True
    EndIf
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  tTook = True
  If NOT tOK Then
    RxNonText = RxNonText + 1
    dropN = tEnd
    Call BufDrop()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxLine = ""
  For tk2 = 1 To tEnd - 1
    RxLine = RxLine & CHR(rxByte(tk2))
  Next tk2
  dropN = tEnd
  Call BufDrop()
  If Len(RxLine) > 0 Then Call ParseLine()
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' DecodeFrame -- one binary frame, from rxByte(frSigAt) to rxByte(frEnd).
'
' Every intermediate is written to a Public and then to FrameLog, because the
' frame layout below is read off three reference frames rather than out of a
' document, and a decode built on an inference has to be able to say what it
' inferred. FrElemVia in particular records WHICH rule found the element.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub DecodeFrame()
  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1
  RxFrameSeq = RxFrameSeq + 1
  FrElemOff = 0
  FrElemVia = 0
  FrElemLen = 0
  FrElemHex = ""
  FrTimeSecs = 0
  FrRecords = 0
  FrGood = 0
  FrBad = 0
  whyCode = 0
  RxLastWhy = ""

  FrHex = ""
  For fk = frSigAt To frEnd
    Sprintf (numStr,"%02X ",rxByte(fk))
    If Len(FrHex) <= 380 Then FrHex = FrHex & numStr
  Next fk
  RxStep = "5 frame captured"

  'The anchor: 84 01 <len> 74. Present on every frame in the reference capture,
  'at the same offset, and the length it carries satisfied (len-3) MOD 4 = 0 on
  'all of them.
  frElemAt = 0
  For fk = frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1 To frEnd - 4
    If rxByte(fk) = 132 Then
      If rxByte(fk + 1) = 1 AND rxByte(fk + 3) = ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
        FrElemLen = rxByte(fk + 2)
        frElemAt = fk + 3
        FrElemVia = 1
        ExitFor
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next fk

  'Fallback: any 0x74 whose preceding byte is a plausible element length. Looser,
  'and reported as such, so a frame that does not carry the anchor still yields
  'its readings instead of yielding nothing.
  If frElemAt = 0 Then
    For fk = frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 2 To frEnd - 6
      If rxByte(fk) = ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
        FrElemLen = rxByte(fk - 1)
        If FrElemLen >= 7 AND FrElemLen <= 32 Then
          If ((FrElemLen - 3) MOD 4) = 0 AND fk + FrElemLen - 1 <= frEnd Then
            frElemAt = fk
            FrElemVia = 2
            ExitFor
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    Next fk
  EndIf

  If frElemAt = 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "no concentration element in frame"
    whyCode = 13
    RxWhyCount(13) = RxWhyCount(13) + 1
    RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
    RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
    CallTable FrameLog
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  FrElemOff = frElemAt - frSigAt + 1
  If FrElemLen < 7 OR FrElemLen > 32 OR ((FrElemLen - 3) MOD 4) <> 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "element length is not whole records"
    whyCode = 14
    RxWhyCount(14) = RxWhyCount(14) + 1
    RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
    RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
    CallTable FrameLog
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  For fk = frElemAt To frElemAt + FrElemLen - 1
    Sprintf (numStr,"%02X ",rxByte(fk))
    If Len(FrElemHex) <= 100 Then FrElemHex = FrElemHex & numStr
  Next fk
  RxStep = "6 element decoded"

  'The frame's own clock, against ours -- the same comparison the ASCII path
  'makes, and the same open question. See ParseAlert2.
  FrTimeSecs = rxByte(frElemAt + 1) * 256 + rxByte(frElemAt + 2)
  loggerSecs = rTime(4) * 3600 + rTime(5) * 60 + rTime(6)
  RxFrameSkew = FrTimeSecs - loggerSecs
  If RxFrameSkew > 43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew - 86400
  If RxFrameSkew < -43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew + 86400

  FrRecords = (FrElemLen - 3) / 4
  RdIndex = 0
  For frRec = frElemAt + 3 To frElemAt + FrElemLen - 1 Step 4
    RdIndex = RdIndex + 1
    RdB0 = rxByte(frRec)
    RdB1 = rxByte(frRec + 1)
    RdB2 = rxByte(frRec + 2)
    RdB3 = rxByte(frRec + 3)
    Sprintf (RdRecHex,"%02X %02X %02X %02X",RdB0,RdB1,RdB2,RdB3)

    'Same unpacking as the ASCII path: the middle byte carries the top three
    'bits of the value and the top five bits of the address.
    RxLastId = (RdB1 MOD 32) * 256 + RdB0
    RxLastValue = INT(RdB1 / 32) * 256 + RdB2
    RxLastRecHex = RdRecHex
    RdQueued = False

    If RdB3 <> 0 Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    ElseIf RxLastId < 1 OR RxLastId > 65535 Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    ElseIf NOT ClockOK Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
      whyCode = 8
      RxWhyCount(8) = RxWhyCount(8) + 1
    Else
      suspect = 0
      If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
      Call QueuePush()
      RdQueued = True
      FrGood = FrGood + 1
    EndIf
    CallTable ReadingLog
  Next frRec

  CallTable FrameLog
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TakeFrames -- everything framable in the byte buffer, until nothing is.
'
' Binary frames first, text lines only when there is no signature to work with.
' The order matters: an ASCII feed's line also begins "ALERT2", so the mode test
' below decides between them on the two bytes after the signature. "ALERT2A,"
' is a letter A followed by a comma; a length byte of 65 followed by 44 would be
' a 71-byte frame whose payload began with a comma, which no observed frame does.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub TakeFrames()
  frLoops = 0
  Do
    frLoops = frLoops + 1
    If frLoops > FRAME_LOOPS Then ExitDo
    frDone = False

    frSigAt = 0
    If nBuf >= SIG_LEN Then
      For fk = 1 To nBuf - SIG_LEN + 1
        If rxByte(fk) = 65 Then
          If rxByte(fk + 1) = 76 AND rxByte(fk + 2) = 69 AND rxByte(fk + 3) = 82 Then
            If rxByte(fk + 4) = 84 AND rxByte(fk + 5) = 50 Then
              frSigAt = fk
              ExitFor
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      Next fk
    EndIf

    'Which form is it? "ALERT2A," is a letter A followed by a comma; a binary
    'frame's byte 7 is a length, and no observed frame has a length of 65 with a
    'comma behind it. Worked out before the chain below so the chain stays a
    'plain If/ElseIf -- there is no loop-continue statement in this program,
    'deliberately, because that is one more construct that cannot be checked
    'here before it reaches a compiler.
    frIsAscii = False
    If frSigAt > 0 Then
      If nBuf >= frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1 Then
        If rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN) = 65 AND rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1) = 44 Then frIsAscii = True
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If frSigAt = 0 Then
      'Nothing framable. Try a text line instead, and stop when a pass consumes
      'nothing -- otherwise an empty run, such as the LF left over from a CRLF,
      'would be found again on every iteration.
      Call TakeTextLine()
      If NOT tTook Then frDone = True

    ElseIf nBuf < frSigAt + SIG_LEN Then
      frDone = True                                'the length byte is not here yet

    ElseIf frIsAscii Then
      RxFrameMode = "ascii"
      Call TakeTextLine()
      If NOT tTook Then frDone = True

    Else
      RxFrameMode = "binary"
      FrLenByte = rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN)
      FrTotal = SIG_LEN + FrLenByte
      frEnd = frSigAt + FrTotal - 1

      If FrLenByte < FRAME_MIN_LEN OR FrTotal > FRAME_MAX Then
        'Not a length. Step past this signature byte and keep looking, rather
        'than trusting it and swallowing everything behind it.
        RxFrameBadLen = RxFrameBadLen + 1
        dropN = frSigAt
        Call BufDrop()
      ElseIf nBuf < frEnd Then
        frDone = True                              'wait for the rest of it
      Else
        FrPre = frSigAt - 1
        Call DecodeFrame()
        dropN = frEnd
        Call BufDrop()
        FrLeftover = nBuf
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If frDone Then ExitDo
  Loop
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ReadSerial -- steps 1 to 3 of the pipeline, once per scan.
'
' SerialInBlock takes everything the port has and returns how many bytes that
' was, which is the count every loop below is bounded by. This is the KDO
' driver's read, and it replaces the SerialInRecord call that made the first
' version of this program silently deaf -- see the note at the top of the file.
'
' SerialFlush is deliberately NOT called here. The KDO driver flushes because it
' owns a request/response conversation and anything left over is stale. This
' program is a listener on a feed it does not drive, and a flush would throw
' away the bytes that arrived while the scan was working.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ReadSerial()
  RxAvail = SerialInChk (RX_PORT)
  RxBlockBytes = SerialInBlock (RX_PORT,RxBuf,RX_BLOCK)

  If RxBlockBytes > 0 Then
    RxBlockSeq = RxBlockSeq + 1
    RxBytesTotal = RxBytesTotal + RxBlockBytes
    SecsSinceRx = 0
    RxStep = "1 bytes read"

    'MoveBytes, not a plain assignment. Assigning one string to another copies
    'up to the first NUL, and this feed carries nine to eleven NULs per frame --
    'so a copy would truncate exactly the data the program exists to read.
    'MoveBytes copies the count it is given and does not care what is in it.
    MoveBytes (dumpSrc,0,RxBuf,0,RxBlockBytes)
    dumpLen = RxBlockBytes
    dumpCensus = True
    Call DumpBytes()
    dumpCensus = False
    RxBlockHex = dumpHex
    RxBlockText = dumpTxt
    RxBlockHexFull = dumpHexFull
    RxStep = "2 block dumped"

    Call CaptureBlock()

    'Into the byte buffer, raw. Every byte, including NUL, including CR -- this
    'is the step the first version got wrong, and getting it wrong here cannot
    'be recovered from anywhere downstream.
    RxBufLenBefore = nBuf
    For dk = 1 To RxBlockBytes
      If nBuf < BUF_MAX Then
        nBuf = nBuf + 1
        rxByte(nBuf) = ASCII(dumpSrc(1,1,dk))
      Else
        RxBufDrops = RxBufDrops + 1
      EndIf
    Next dk
    RxStep = "3 appended to byte buffer"
    CallTable RawLog
  Else
    SecsSinceRx = SecsSinceRx + 1
    RxBlockHex = ""
    RxBlockText = ""
  EndIf

  Call TakeFrames()

  'What is still waiting, shown both ways.
  RxBufLen = nBuf
  dumpLen = nBuf
  If dumpLen > 250 Then dumpLen = 250
  For dk = 1 To dumpLen
    MoveBytes (dumpSrc,dk - 1,CHR(rxByte(dk)),0,1)
  Next dk
  dumpCensus = False
  Call DumpBytes()
  RxBufHex = dumpHex
  RxBufText = dumpTxt
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' BuildBatch -- up to MAX_BATCH readings out of the ring buffer and into
' ContentsString, without removing them. BatchN says how many went in.
'
' Two callers, one body, one difference between them, and it is the wrapper:
'
'   HTTP  {"payload":{"path":..,"protocol":..,"readings":[..]}}
'   MQTT            {"path":..,"protocol":..,"readings":[..]}
'
' PostgREST maps an RPC body's top-level keys to the function's arguments, so
' ingest_http() -- whose one argument is called `payload` -- has to be handed an
' object with a `payload` key. The bridge calls that same function itself and
' builds the wrapper on this station's behalf, so a message that arrived
' carrying one would be a payload nested inside a payload. Hence bWrap, set by
' the caller: the same readings, addressed to the two things that receive them.
'
' bFrom is the tail to start from, because the two paths are at different points
' in the buffer whenever one of them is behind.
'
' Nothing leaves the queue here. Readings are only released once the path in
' question has confirmed them -- the endpoint saying it stored them, or the
' broker returning a PUBACK -- which is what makes a failed attempt cost nothing
' and a timeout cost one duplicate at worst.
'
' The length guard is what actually bounds the body -- MAX_BATCH is the second
' line of defence. Overflow behaviour on a CRBasic string is not something to
' rely on either way, and a truncated JSON body is a 400 for the whole batch, so
' the check happens before the append rather than after it. The guard is the
' same for both paths: the MQTT message is two characters shorter than the POST
' body and the broker's own ceiling is 256 KiB, so this buffer is the only bound
' that ever binds.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub BuildBatch()
  'The envelope, rebuilt here rather than once in BeginProg, because BASE_NAME
  'is Public and a name changed at the logger has to reach the next POST.
  '
  'Quotes, backslashes and control characters are replaced on the way in. The
  'name lands inside a JSON string literal, and one of those typed into a Public
  'field would turn every batch into a 400 with nothing on the logger to say
  'why -- a failure the Const version could not have.
  jName = ""
  For nk2 = 1 To Len(BASE_NAME)
    nc2 = ASCII(Mid(BASE_NAME,nk2,1))
    If nc2 = 34 OR nc2 = 92 OR nc2 < 32 Then
      jName = jName & "_"
    Else
      jName = jName & CHR(nc2)
    EndIf
  Next nk2

  jEnvelope = DQ & "path" & DQ & ":" & DQ & jName & DQ & ","
  jEnvelope = jEnvelope & DQ & "protocol" & DQ & ":" & DQ & PROTOCOL & DQ & ","
  jEnvelope = jEnvelope & DQ & "readings" & DQ & ":["

  If bWrap Then
    ContentsString = "{" & DQ & "payload" & DQ & ":{" & jEnvelope
  Else
    ContentsString = "{" & jEnvelope
  EndIf

  BatchN = 0
  bi = bFrom

  Do While bi <> QHead AND BatchN < MAX_BATCH
    mi = bi
    Call MakeRec()
    If BatchN > 0 Then jRec = "," & jRec

    If Len(ContentsString) + Len(jRec) > CONTENT_GUARD Then ExitDo

    ContentsString = ContentsString & jRec
    BatchN = BatchN + 1
    bi = bi + 1
    If bi > Q_SIZE Then bi = 1
  Loop

  If bWrap Then
    ContentsString = ContentsString & jTail
  Else
    ContentsString = ContentsString & jTailBare
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' JsonInt -- the integer after "<jsonKey>": in HTTPResponse, into jsonVal.
' -1 if the key is not there.
'
' A full JSON parser is not what this needs. The response is a flat object
' written by one function and the numbers this program acts on are never nested.
'
' It does have to step over whitespace, and that is not a detail. Postgres
' renders jsonb with a space after every colon:
'
'   {"accepted": 1, "duplicates": 0, "rejected": [], "raw_id": 4821}
'
' An earlier version started reading digits at the character straight after the
' colon, hit that space, stopped, and returned the zero it had already stored --
' so every POST reported "accepted: 0" and, by subtraction, every reading in it
' rejected. The data was being stored correctly the whole time; only the
' counters were wrong, which is the worst way for a diagnostic to fail. The
' example in docs/ingest-http.md shows the spaces, and had been all along.
'
' jsonVal is now only set to 0 once a digit is actually present, so "key exists
' but its value is not a number" reports -1 rather than reporting zero.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub JsonInt()
  jsonVal = -1
  srcStr = HTTPResponse
  needle = DQ & jsonKey & DQ & ":"
  Call FindStr()
  If foundAt > 0 Then
    jp = foundAt + Len(needle)
    Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
      jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
      If jd <> 32 AND jd <> 9 Then ExitDo
      jp = jp + 1
    Loop

    jd = 0
    If jp <= Len(srcStr) Then jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
    If jd >= 48 AND jd <= 57 Then
      jsonVal = 0
      Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
        jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
        If jd < 48 OR jd > 57 Then ExitDo
        jsonVal = jsonVal * 10 + (jd - 48)
        jp = jp + 1
      Loop
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ParseStatus -- the HTTP status code out of RespHeader, into LastStatus.
'
' There is no status-code parameter on HTTPPost: it returns a socket. The status
' arrives in the header variable, because the instruction writes the server's
' response headers back over whatever request headers it was given -- which is
' also why PostBatch rebuilds them every single time.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ParseStatus()
  LastStatus = 0
  srcStr = RespHeader
  needle = "HTTP/"
  Call FindStr()
  If foundAt > 0 Then
    pp = foundAt
    Do While pp <= Len(srcStr)                   'step over "HTTP/1.1"
      If Mid(srcStr,pp,1) = " " Then ExitDo
      pp = pp + 1
    Loop
    pp = pp + 1
    Do While pp <= Len(srcStr)
      pd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,pp,1))
      If pd < 48 OR pd > 57 Then ExitDo
      LastStatus = LastStatus * 10 + (pd - 48)
      pp = pp + 1
    Loop
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' LoadToken -- the secret, out of TOKEN_FILE.
'
' Kept out of this program so the file can live in a public repository and be
' loaded onto a second base station without carrying a live credential -- which
' is what docs/ingest-http.md asks for in as many words. Kept out of the Public
' table for the same reason: a Public token is readable by anyone who can reach
' the logger.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub LoadToken()
  IngestToken = ""
  TokenOK = False
  TokenLen = 0
  fh = FileOpen (TOKEN_FILE,"r",0)
  If fh <> 0 Then
    FileReadLine (fh,IngestToken,80)
    FileClose (fh)

    'Trim whatever the editor that wrote the file left on the end. A trailing
    'newline here is not cosmetic: it would terminate the header block early and
    'the three headers after it would never be sent.
    Do While Len(IngestToken) > 0
      ltk = ASCII(Mid(IngestToken,Len(IngestToken),1))
      If ltk = 13 OR ltk = 10 OR ltk = 32 OR ltk = 9 Then
        IngestToken = Mid(IngestToken,1,Len(IngestToken) - 1)
      Else
        ExitDo
      EndIf
    Loop

    TokenLen = Len(IngestToken)
    If TokenLen > 0 Then TokenOK = True
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PostBatch -- one POST, and what came back.
'
' Success is "the response says accepted", not "the socket was non-zero". The
' endpoint never answers 204 for exactly this reason: docs/ingest-http.md's rule
' is that a logger which cannot tell stored from silently dropped produces gaps
' nobody notices for a month. So the socket and the status code are recorded as
' diagnostics, and the response body is what decides whether the queue advances.
'
' The header is rebuilt on every call, not once in BeginProg. HTTPPost writes
' the server's response headers back into the same string it was given, so a
' header variable built once is correct exactly once -- on the second post the
' apikey and the token would have been replaced by whatever the server said.
' SentHeader keeps a redacted copy of what actually went out.
'
' HTTPPost is called with its four required parameters and nothing else. The
' optional TimeOut is the ninth, and reaching it means passing four empty
' placeholders for the table-streaming parameters in between -- placeholders
' this program cannot compile to check. The default timeout is 75 seconds, which
' is generous rather than tight and costs nothing here: a long block delays only
' this sequence, and the scan reading the serial port is a different one.
'
' Nothing here writes a request line or a Content-Length: HTTPPost prepends the
' one and appends the other, and a program that adds them too sends them twice.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub PostBatch()
  PostAttempts = PostAttempts + 1

  HTTPHeader = "apikey: " & API_KEY & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "X-Ingest-Token: " & IngestToken & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "Content-Type: application/json" & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "Content-Profile: meganet" & CRLF

  'What went out, with the credential replaced by its length -- enough to spot a
  'truncated paste or a missing file, without putting the token on screen.
  Sprintf (pNum,"%d",TokenLen)
  SentHeader = "apikey: " & API_KEY
  SentHeader = SentHeader & " | X-Ingest-Token: " & pNum & " chars"
  SentHeader = SentHeader & " | Content-Type: application/json"
  SentHeader = SentHeader & " | Content-Profile: meganet"

  HTTPResponse = ""
  LastPayload = ContentsString

  LastSocket = HTTPPost (INGEST_URI,ContentsString,HTTPResponse,HTTPHeader)

  RespHeader = HTTPHeader                        'the response headers, now
  Call ParseStatus()

  jsonKey = "accepted"
  Call JsonInt()

  If jsonVal >= 0 Then
    LastAccepted = jsonVal
    Accepted = Accepted + jsonVal

    jsonKey = "duplicates"
    Call JsonInt()
    If jsonVal < 0 Then jsonVal = 0
    LastDuplicates = jsonVal
    Duplicates = Duplicates + jsonVal

    'Every reading in a batch is accepted, a duplicate, or rejected -- the
    'contract leaves no fourth outcome -- so the rejected count is arithmetic
    'and the array does not need counting. What the array is read for is the
    'first reason, because the reasons repeat and docs/ingest-http.md tabulates
    'what each one usually means.
    LastRejected = BatchN - LastAccepted - LastDuplicates
    If LastRejected < 0 Then LastRejected = 0
    Rejected = Rejected + LastRejected

    LastReject = ""
    If LastRejected > 0 Then
      'Same whitespace problem as JsonInt: the rendered body is `"why": "..."`,
      'so the needle stops at the key and the colon, spaces and opening quote
      'are stepped over here.
      srcStr = HTTPResponse
      needle = DQ & "why" & DQ
      Call FindStr()
      If foundAt > 0 Then
        jp = foundAt + Len(needle)
        Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
          jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
          If jd <> 58 AND jd <> 32 AND jd <> 9 AND jd <> 34 Then ExitDo
          jp = jp + 1
        Loop
        LastReject = Mid(HTTPResponse,jp,150)
      EndIf
    EndIf

    'Stored. Release exactly what was sent, from THIS path's tail -- QHead may
    'have moved on since, and the publisher's tail is none of this sequence's
    'business at this point in the scan.
    QTailHttp = QTailHttp + BatchN
    Do While QTailHttp > Q_SIZE
      QTailHttp = QTailHttp - Q_SIZE
    Loop

    PostOK = PostOK + 1
    ConsecFail = 0
    Backoff = 0
    SecsSincePost = 0
    If LastRejected > 0 Then
      PostState = "accepted, some rejected"
    Else
      PostState = "accepted"
    EndIf

  Else
    'No "accepted" in the body. Either the request never landed, or the endpoint
    'refused it -- 401 for a token it does not know, 400 for a body it could not
    'read. Both come back as a JSON error object, so the first characters of it
    'are worth more than any code this program could invent.
    PostFail = PostFail + 1
    ConsecFail = ConsecFail + 1
    LastReject = Mid(HTTPResponse,1,150)
    LastAccepted = 0
    LastDuplicates = 0
    LastRejected = 0

    If LastSocket = 0 Then
      PostState = "no answer - check link and TLS"
    ElseIf LastSocket = -2 Then
      PostState = "HTTPPost did not execute"
    ElseIf LastStatus = 401 Then
      PostState = "refused - token"
    ElseIf LastStatus = 400 Then
      PostState = "refused - malformed body"
    ElseIf LastStatus > 0 Then
      Sprintf (pNum,"%d",LastStatus)
      PostState = "refused - HTTP " & pNum
    Else
      PostState = "no usable answer - see RespHeader"
    EndIf

    If Backoff = 0 Then
      Backoff = BACKOFF_MIN
    Else
      Backoff = Backoff * 2
      If Backoff > BACKOFF_MAX Then Backoff = BACKOFF_MAX
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' MqttFailed / MqttWorked -- what a refused or accepted publish costs, in one
' place, because BOTH messages this program sends are evidence about the same
' broker connection. A status message that got through says the readings can go
' too; readings that got through say the station is in a position to say hello.
' Two separate backoffs would have each path relearning the same fact.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub MqttFailed()
  MqttFail = MqttFail + 1
  MqttConsecFail = MqttConsecFail + 1

  'The session is gone or was never made, so whatever the broker is holding as
  'this station's retained status is now the Last Will. Re-publishing it is how
  'the station says it is back, and there is no connect event in CRBasic to hang
  'that on.
  statusDue = True

  If MqttBackoff = 0 Then
    MqttBackoff = MQTT_BACKOFF_MIN
  Else
    MqttBackoff = MqttBackoff * 2
    If MqttBackoff > MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX Then MqttBackoff = MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub MqttWorked()
  MqttConsecFail = 0
  MqttBackoff = 0
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PublishBatch -- one MQTT message, and what the broker did with it.
'
' Called immediately after BuildBatch has built the unwrapped envelope, in the
' same sequence and with nothing in between: it takes the batch size out of
' BatchN, which the next BuildBatch of either path will overwrite.
'
' MQTTPublish returns 0 for success and its own error code otherwise. At QoS 1
' that success means the broker sent a PUBACK, so this is a genuine
' acknowledgement from the far end of the link and not merely "the instruction
' ran" -- which is exactly the distinction docs/ingest-http.md insists on for the
' other path, and the reason the tail advances here rather than optimistically.
'
' What it does NOT mean is that MegaNet has the reading. MQTT gives a publisher
' no response channel: the PUBACK is the broker's, and the bridge only acks a
' storable message to the broker once the database has actually stored it, so a
' bridge or database outage leaves the message queued at the broker rather than
' losing it. That is the contract's guarantee and there is nothing this program
' can or should do to check it -- which is why MqttPublished counts readings
' handed over, and is named for that rather than for readings stored.
'
' Nothing here is retried by hand. A failure leaves the tail where it is, and
' the next attempt rebuilds the same batch from the same slot.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub PublishBatch()
  MqttAttempts = MqttAttempts + 1
  MqttLastBatch = BatchN
  MqttLastPayload = Mid(ContentsString,1,200)
  SecsSinceTry = 0

  MqttResult = MQTTPublish (MqttTopic,ContentsString,MQTT_QOS,MQTT_DROP)

  If MqttResult = 0 Then
    'Handed over. Release exactly what was published, from this path's tail.
    QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt + MqttLastBatch
    Do While QTailMqtt > Q_SIZE
      QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt - Q_SIZE
    Loop

    MqttOK = MqttOK + 1
    MqttPublished = MqttPublished + MqttLastBatch
    SecsSinceMqtt = 0
    MqttState = "published"
    Call MqttWorked()
  Else
    Sprintf (mNum,"%d",MqttResult)
    MqttState = "broker refused or unreachable - code " & mNum
    Call MqttFailed()
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PublishStatus -- the retained "I am here" message.
'
' Half of the offline detection. The other half is the Last Will, which this
' program cannot set: it is a Device Configuration Utility setting (Last Will
' Topic, Message, QoS and Retained), the broker publishes it on this station's
' behalf when the connection drops without a clean disconnect, and it has to
' name the SAME topic as MqttStatusTopic below and be marked retained. A will
' left at the default "Do Not Retain" is a will that vanishes the moment the
' bridge reconnects, which is the failure this whole mechanism exists to survive.
' logger/README.md lists the settings; docs/ingest-mqtt.md explains the shape.
'
' Retained, so the broker replays it to the bridge on every reconnect and a
' bridge restart does not lose the picture. Anything beyond `online` is kept
' verbatim in meganet.station_status.last_status, so a field added here needs no
' migration and no bridge release -- which is why the firmware version is worth
' sending: "which loggers are still on the old program" becomes a query.
'
' Batt_volt is range-tested rather than trusted. A NAN fails both halves of the
' test, the same way the NTP result is tested in the clock sequence, and the
' field is dropped rather than sent -- the word NAN inside a JSON number is a
' message the bridge can only reject, and it would be rejected retained.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub PublishStatus()
  MqttStatusBody = "{" & DQ & "online" & DQ & ":true"
  If Batt_volt > 0 AND Batt_volt < 100 Then
    Sprintf (mNum,"%.2f",Batt_volt)
    MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "," & DQ & "battery_v" & DQ & ":" & mNum
  EndIf
  MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "," & DQ & "fw" & DQ & ":" & DQ & FW_VERSION & DQ
  MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "}"

  MqttAttempts = MqttAttempts + 1
  SecsSinceTry = 0

  MqttStatusResult = MQTTPublish (MqttStatusTopic,MqttStatusBody,MQTT_QOS,MQTT_KEEP)

  If MqttStatusResult = 0 Then
    SecsSinceStatus = 0
    statusDue = False
    Call MqttWorked()
  Else
    'Counted into the same backoff as a failed batch, and this is the case that
    'needs it most: a base station with an empty queue has no readings to knock
    'on the broker with, so this message IS the probe, and an un-backed-off
    'probe is a blocking publish every slow scan for as long as the broker is
    'down. MqttState is left saying whatever the readings last said, because a
    'failed status message adds nothing a failed batch has not already said.
    Call MqttFailed()
  EndIf
EndSub


'==============================================================================
' 6. MAIN PROGRAM
'==============================================================================

BeginProg

  DQ = CHR(34)
  CRLF = CHR(13) & CHR(10)

  'The per-reading JSON fragments, built once. The comment beside each
  'declaration above shows what it contains. jEnvelope is NOT built here -- it
  'carries BASE_NAME, which is Public, so BuildBatch rebuilds it per POST.
  '
  '`source` is deliberately absent from the envelope: ingest_http() stamps it
  '"http" itself, and a value repeated here could only ever disagree with the
  'truth.
  jTail = "]}}"
  jTailBare = "]}"
  fId   = "{" & DQ & "alert_id" & DQ & ":"
  fTs   = "," & DQ & "reading_ts" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fVal  = DQ & "," & DQ & "value_raw" & DQ & ":"
  fSus  = "," & DQ & "quality" & DQ & ":" & DQ & "suspect" & DQ

  'The topics, built once from the two segments in section 1 rather than typed
  'out, so the scheme appears in this program exactly once. They are Public: at
  'commissioning, reading MqttTopic off the logger and checking it against the
  'line in the broker's ACL is the whole of the topic check, and it is the check
  'worth doing before anyone drives home -- a wrong segment is refused by the
  'broker rather than filed under an identity nobody claims.
  MqttTopic = "meganet/v1/" & MQTT_STATION & "/" & MQTT_DEVICE & "/reading"
  MqttStatusTopic = "meganet/v1/" & MQTT_STATION & "/status"

  QHead = 1
  QTailHttp = 1
  QTailMqtt = 1
  QDepth = 0
  QDepthHttp = 0
  QDepthMqtt = 0
  Backoff = 0
  ConsecFail = 0
  MqttBackoff = 0
  MqttConsecFail = 0
  SecsSinceTry = MQTT_EVERY          'so the first status message goes out on the
                                     'first slow scan rather than 30 seconds in
  nBuf = 0
  RxFrameMode = "unknown"
  RxStep = "waiting for the first byte"
  PostState = "starting"

  'Say hello as soon as there is a session to say it on. A restart is exactly
  'when the broker is still holding a will that says this station is down.
  statusDue = True
  If MQTT_ENABLE Then
    MqttState = "starting"
  Else
    MqttState = "disabled - see MQTT_ENABLE"
  EndIf

  'Labels for the two census arrays, so they read as themselves in the Public
  'table instead of as eight and eleven anonymous numbers.
  RxByteClassName(1) = "NUL"
  RxByteClassName(2) = "ctrl-other"
  RxByteClassName(3) = "CR"
  RxByteClassName(4) = "LF"
  RxByteClassName(5) = "TAB"
  RxByteClassName(6) = "space"
  RxByteClassName(7) = "printable"
  RxByteClassName(8) = "high >126"

  RxWhyName(1)  = "1 short line"
  RxWhyName(2)  = "2 frame flagged invalid"
  RxWhyName(3)  = "3 payload len vs hex"
  RxWhyName(4)  = "4 payload size"
  RxWhyName(5)  = "5 not whole records"
  RxWhyName(6)  = "6 payload not hex"
  RxWhyName(7)  = "7 not concentration elem"
  RxWhyName(8)  = "8 clock not set"
  RxWhyName(9)  = "9 alert id out of range"
  RxWhyName(10) = "10 unrecognised line"
  RxWhyName(11) = "11 no line terminator"
  RxWhyName(12) = "12 fields are not numbers"
  RxWhyName(13) = "13 no element in frame"
  RxWhyName(14) = "14 element len not records"

  Call LoadToken()

  'Once, here -- not in the scan. Re-opening the port every second re-allocates
  'the ring buffer and resets its pointers, discarding whatever arrived since
  'the last call, which on a busy base station is most of it. Same five
  'parameters, in the same order, as the KDO driver uses.
  SerialOpen (RX_PORT,RX_BAUD,RX_FORMAT,RX_TXDELAY,RX_BUFFER)
  SerialFlush (RX_PORT)

  'The raw capture starts itself on every program start, so a logger that was
  'power-cycled at site comes back capturing rather than waiting to be told.
  'Set CaptureNow false to stop it; the file stays either way.
  CaptureNow = True
  Call StampNow()
  Call CapStart()

  '--- the fast half: listen --------------------------------------------------
  Scan (1,Sec,3,0)

    'PanelTemp's second parameter is the notch frequency -- the interference this
    'measurement rejects -- so it is the local mains frequency, 50 in Australia.
    PanelTemp (PTemp,50)
    Battery (Batt_volt)
    WatchdogErrs = Status.WatchdogErrors
    SkippedScans = Status.SkippedScan
    VarOutOfBound = Status.VarOutofBound

    Call StampNow()
    Call ReadSerial()

    'Capture housekeeping. The flush is time-based as well as size-based so a
    'quiet feed still commits what it has, and CaptureReset clears itself once
    'acted on so it behaves like a button rather than a mode.
    If CaptureReset Then
      CaptureReset = False
      Call CapStart()
    EndIf
    capSecs = capSecs + 1
    If capSecs >= CAP_FLUSH_SEC Then Call CapFlush()

    'How far behind each path is, and how much the buffer is therefore still
    'holding. QDepth is the greater of the two because a slot is only reusable
    'once BOTH have passed it -- so it, and not either half, is what QPeak and
    'Q_SIZE have to be read against.
    QDepthHttp = QHead - QTailHttp
    If QDepthHttp < 0 Then QDepthHttp = QDepthHttp + Q_SIZE
    QDepthMqtt = QHead - QTailMqtt
    If QDepthMqtt < 0 Then QDepthMqtt = QDepthMqtt + Q_SIZE

    If MQTT_ENABLE Then
      qLag = QDepthMqtt
    Else
      'Nothing is draining the publisher's tail, so counting it would report a
      'buffer that only fills. QueuePush still moves that tail out of the way.
      qLag = 0
    EndIf
    QDepth = QDepthHttp
    If qLag > QDepth Then QDepth = qLag
    If QDepth > QPeak Then QPeak = QDepth

    SecsSincePost = SecsSincePost + 1
    SecsSinceMqtt = SecsSinceMqtt + 1
    SecsSinceTry = SecsSinceTry + 1
    SecsSinceStatus = SecsSinceStatus + 1

    CallTable Diag

  NextScan

  '--- the slow half: talk, both ways -----------------------------------------
  '
  ' A sequence of its own because BOTH of the instructions in it block: HTTPPost
  ' sits there until the server answers or its 75-second timeout expires, and
  ' MQTTPublish at QoS 1 waits for the broker's PUBACK. A base station that stops
  ' reading its serial port for that long has lost every transmission in the
  ' window. Nothing in here touches the receiver, and the only state it shares
  ' with the scan above is the ring buffer.
  '
  ' ONE sequence for both paths rather than one each, and that is the load-
  ' bearing decision in this file. Two sequences would let a slow POST and a slow
  ' publish overlap, and the two halves share BuildBatch, ContentsString, BatchN
  ' and MakeRec's scratch -- so overlapping them means a message occasionally
  ' built out of half of the other path's batch. That is the failure mode this
  ' program spends its scratch-variable comments avoiding, and it would be
  ' invisible: a well-formed batch of the wrong readings.
  '
  ' What it costs is that a stalled POST delays a publish by up to 75 seconds.
  ' What it does not cost is progress, which is the thing that matters: the two
  ' tails are independent, so the delayed path resumes from where it was and
  ' catches up in batches of MAX_BATCH. An HTTP outage does not stop the
  ' publishing, and a broker outage does not stop the POSTs.
  SlowSequence
    Scan (10,Sec,3,0)

      '--- path 1: POST ---------------------------------------------------
      postDue = False
      If QDepthHttp > 0 Then
        If ConsecFail = 0 Then
          If SecsSincePost >= POST_EVERY Then postDue = True
        Else
          If SecsSincePost >= Backoff Then postDue = True
        EndIf
      EndIf

      If NOT TokenOK Then
        PostState = "no token - see TOKEN_FILE"
        Call LoadToken()                 'so dropping the file in is enough,
        postDue = False                  'with no restart
      ElseIf NOT ClockOK Then
        PostState = "clock not set - nothing stamped"
        postDue = False
      ElseIf QDepthHttp = 0 Then
        PostState = "idle - nothing to send"
      EndIf

      If postDue Then
        bWrap = True                     'ingest_http() wants the payload wrapper
        bFrom = QTailHttp
        Call BuildBatch()
        If BatchN > 0 Then
          PostState = "posting"
          Call PostBatch()
        EndIf
      EndIf

      '--- path 2: PUBLISH ------------------------------------------------
      '
      ' No token test here, and that is not an omission: the bridge holds the
      ' ingest token for this path, and what this station needs instead is a
      ' broker credential, which lives in the MQTT settings where CRBasic cannot
      ' see it. A publish that fails for want of one fails at MQTTPublish and
      ' says so in MqttState.
      If MQTT_ENABLE Then
        'The status message is due on its own timer, and after any failure --
        'armed here whether or not the broker is going to be touched this tick,
        'so that arming it never depends on the thing it is waiting for.
        If SecsSinceStatus >= MQTT_STATUS_EVERY Then statusDue = True

        'ONE gate over both messages: how long the broker is left alone. Both
        'are blocking publishes to the same connection, so letting either past
        'the backoff on its own would be a knock every ten seconds at exactly
        'the moment the link can least carry one.
        '
        'Measured from the last attempt rather than the last success, because a
        'backoff measured from a success that has not happened for an hour has
        'already expired every time it is read.
        mqttWait = MQTT_EVERY
        If MqttConsecFail > 0 Then mqttWait = MqttBackoff

        If SecsSinceTry >= mqttWait Then
          mqttSent = False

          If NOT ClockOK Then
            'Belt and braces: QueuePush will not have queued anything unstamped.
            MqttState = "clock not set - nothing stamped"
          ElseIf QDepthMqtt > 0 Then
            bWrap = False                'the bridge builds the wrapper itself
            bFrom = QTailMqtt
            Call BuildBatch()
            If BatchN > 0 Then
              MqttState = "publishing"
              Call PublishBatch()
              mqttSent = True
            EndIf
          Else
            MqttState = "idle - nothing to send"
          EndIf

          'Readings first, then the status -- but only when the tick has not
          'already spent a blocking publish on a broker that refused it. The two
          'ways through are the two things this message is for:
          '
          '  nothing was sent    -- it is the probe that finds out the broker is
          '                         back, on a station with no readings to find
          '                         that out with
          '  a batch just landed -- the broker is demonstrably up, so say the
          '                         station is here NOW rather than after the
          '                         backlog drains, which is minutes of a
          '                         station showing offline while it is plainly
          '                         posting readings
          If statusDue Then
            If NOT mqttSent OR MqttConsecFail = 0 Then Call PublishStatus()
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf

    NextScan
  EndSequence

  '--- clock discipline -------------------------------------------------------
  '
  ' Its own sequence because NetworkTimeProtocol blocks for up to 70 seconds,
  ' which would stall either of the two above. Five minutes between scans is
  ' comfortably longer than that, so this never overruns.
  '
  ' The return value is the clock error in milliseconds BEFORE the correction,
  ' and NAN when the sync failed. A NAN fails both halves of the range test
  ' below, which is why the test is written as a range rather than as a
  ' comparison against zero -- a good sync legitimately returns near zero, so
  ' testing for zero would conflate success with failure.
  '
  ' If your OS does not have NetworkTimeProtocol, delete this whole sequence and
  ' set the clock by hand or from LoggerNet; nothing else depends on it.
  SlowSequence
    Scan (300,Sec,1,0)
      SecsSinceNTP = SecsSinceNTP + 300
      If Len(NTP_SERVER) > 0 Then
        'NOT ClockVerified is what makes the first pass sync immediately and
        'keeps retrying every five minutes until it works. Waiting for the
        'NTP_EVERY_HR boundary is what let a logger run for hours on a clock
        'nothing had checked.
        If TimeIntoInterval(0,NTP_EVERY_HR,Hr) OR NOT ClockVerified OR NOT ClockOK Then
          NTPErrMs = NetworkTimeProtocol (NTP_SERVER,0,1000)
          If NTPErrMs > -86400000 AND NTPErrMs < 86400000 Then
            NTPLastOK = True
            ClockVerified = True
            SecsSinceNTP = 0
          Else
            NTPLastOK = False
            ClockState = "NTP sync failed - see NTP_SERVER"
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    NextScan
  EndSequence

EndProg


'==============================================================================
' Other dataloggers
'==============================================================================
'
' CR1000X -- save as .CR1X. Check PanelTemp's second parameter against that
'   logger's help: on the CR300 it is the notch frequency in Hz, and on other
'   loggers the same position is an integration time or a _50Hz/_60Hz constant.
'   Check the Status fields too -- the CR1000X has more of them, including the
'   MemoryFree and SkippedSlowScan this program left out for the CR300's sake.
' CR6 -- save as .CR6. The RS-232 port is ComRS232; a serial daughterboard port
'   is ComC1 or ComC3 depending on how it is wired.
'
' Both have more memory than a CR300, so Q_SIZE, RX_BUFFER and CONTENT_SIZE can
' go up. Raise CONTENT_SIZE, CONTENT_GUARD and MAX_BATCH together -- a reading
' is up to 92 characters -- and remember the ceiling is 1000 readings per
' message on BOTH paths: the endpoint's own limit, and the one bridge/ matches
' it with so that a station gets a clear answer rather than a 400 relayed to
' nobody. Q_SIZE is now read by two paths, so size it against the longer of the
' two outages you want to survive, not against either one.
'
' MQTTPublish() is the one instruction here that is not on every operating
' system. It IS on a CR300: the product manual (archive/cr300.pdf, section 13.3,
' printed page 149) lists it among the internet-communications instructions. A
' logger too old for it will not compile this file at all, and MQTT_ENABLE
' cannot help -- the compiler reads the whole program. On such a logger delete
' MqttFailed, MqttWorked, PublishBatch, PublishStatus and the "path 2" block in
' the slow sequence, and the HTTP half is exactly the program it was before.
'
' What that manual does NOT carry is the instruction reference -- section 13.3
' defers to the CRBasic help -- so the four parameters below are taken from
' Campbell's published documentation rather than from a page anyone has read.
' Check them in CRBasic Editor's own help when you compile; it is two call
' sites and nothing else in this program depends on the answer.
'
'==============================================================================
' What this program deliberately does not do
'==============================================================================
'
' It does not convert counts to millimetres or metres. See point 4 at the top.
'
' It does not flush the serial port after reading it. A flush belongs to a
'   driver that owns a request/response conversation, like the KDO one this
'   program's serial handling is modelled on -- there, anything still in the
'   buffer after a reply is stale by definition. Here the receiver talks
'   whenever it likes and a flush would discard the bytes that arrived while
'   the scan was working.
'
' It does not decode HFEM. An HFEM line (":HS=1|I1=..|NN:") is a different
'   format from a different kind of station, and hfem.js is the decoder for it
'   -- one decoder, so there is never a second opinion about what T3 means. A
'   base station relaying HFEM publishes the raw line to the bridge's own
'   .../reading/hfem topic and lets hfem.js decode it there.
'
' It does not stream a DataTable through HTTPPost's optional parameters. Those
'   produce TOB1, TOA5, CSIXML or CSIJSON, and ingest_http() accepts none of
'   them -- it wants one JSON object with a `payload` key.
'
' It does not use MQTTPublishTable, or the logger's own automatic publishing.
'   Those emit CSIJSON or GeoJSON on the MQTTBaseTopic, and the bridge subscribes
'   to a topic scheme and a payload contract that is neither. Leave the automatic
'   publishing turned off: everything it would send is a message the bridge acks,
'   logs as unparseable and counts as rejected. MQTTPublish, which this program
'   uses, takes a topic of its own and does not inherit MQTTBaseTopic.
'
' It does not set the Last Will. There is no CRBasic instruction for it -- the
'   will is sent in the CONNECT packet, which the operating system builds from
'   the MQTT settings. All this program can do is publish the other half of the
'   pair, and say loudly here and in logger/README.md that a base station whose
'   will is unset or left un-retained has no offline detection at all, which is
'   most of what MQTT was added for.
'
' It does not subscribe to anything. The bridge's ACL gives a station `write`
'   and not `readwrite`, so there is nothing to read; commands to stations would
'   be a second topic branch and an explicit widening of that rule, not a
'   quiet one.
'
' It does not call MQTTConnect. The operating system opens and holds the session
'   on its own once MQTT is enabled in the settings, and reconnects it; the
'   instruction exists to FORCE an attempt, which is for programs that power a
'   modem down between transmissions. This base station is mains-powered and
'   listening continuously, so there is nothing to force. A logger that switches
'   its modem on the hour wants MQTTConnect after the link is up, and a PingIP
'   before it -- that is a different program, not a setting on this one.
'
' It does not enforce which addresses this base may post for. Neither does the
'   endpoint: alert_low/alert_high on the token record are a note, not a rule
'   (db/migrations/0007_ingest_http.sql, decision 3).
